eratorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481440 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}(x+y)(x-y)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})\\,du \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}(x+y)(x-y)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})\\,du \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481560 :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m_1 &= \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \\\\ &= \\frac{-1-(5)}{-3-(1)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(5,6).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(6) &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x-(5)) \\\\ y-(6) &= \\frac{10}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2 x}{3} + y &= \\frac{28}{3} \\\\ 3(\\frac{2 x}{3} + y) &= 3(\\frac{28}{3}) \\\\ 2 x + 3 y &= 28\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m_1 &= \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \\\\ &= \\frac{-1-(5)}{-3-(1)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(5,6).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(6) &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x-(5)) \\\\ y-(6) &= \\frac{10}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2 x}{3} + y &= \\frac{28}{3} \\\\ 3(\\frac{2 x}{3} + y) &= 3\\left(\\frac{28}{3}\\right) \\\\ 2 x + 3 y &= 28\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481663 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${r}^{2}$ and $100$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{100}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{10}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{r\\cdot r}{10\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{r}{10}\\cdot \\frac{r}{10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{r}{10})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{r}{10})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{r}{10}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|r|}{|10|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|r|}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${r}^{2}$ and $100$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{100}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{10}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{r\\cdot r}{10\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{r}{10}\\cdot \\frac{r}{10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{r}{10}\\right)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{r}{10}\\right)}^{2}} & =|\\frac{r}{10}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|r|}{|10|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|r|}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481667 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11})≈{58.52}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11}\\right)≈{58.52}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186482511 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps: \n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps: \n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482515 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3,\\,-v\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ -y & -x & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\  & =-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v(w+v)\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3,\\,-v\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ -y & -x & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\  & =-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v(w+v)\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482517 :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}6{e}^{-6y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-6y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-6m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -6m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -6m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}6{e}^{-6y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-6y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-6m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -6m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ -6m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482523 :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2ap+{a}^{2}=(p-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}p+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(p-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2ap+{a}^{2}=(p-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}p+{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(p-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482529 :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+2an+{a}^{2}=(n+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+\\frac{25}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}n+{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=\\frac{5}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(n+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+2an+{a}^{2}=(n+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+\\frac{25}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}n+{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=\\frac{5}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(n+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482530 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u]_{u=\\pi }^{u=2\\pi }\\cdot {[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]}_{v=0}^{v=\\pi }\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(-1+(-1))\\cdot (-(-1)+1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u]_{u=\\pi }^{u=2\\pi }\\cdot {[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]}_{v=0}^{v=\\pi }\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(-1+(-1))\\cdot (-(-1)+1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482534 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(x-0) \\\\  & =3x \\\\  & =3u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}y+3xyz)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(x-0) \\\\  & =3x \\\\  & =3u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}y+3xyz)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482540 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4,\\,3\\le w\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-2y+2x) \\\\  & =6(x-y) \\\\  & =6u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}2w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{6u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4,\\,3\\le w\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-2y+2x) \\\\  & =6(x-y) \\\\  & =6u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}2w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{6u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482547 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=x-z,\\,w=\\frac{z-1}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-2\\le v\\le 0,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{z-1}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=u.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |u|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{5}u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{0}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}dw \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\cdot [v]_{v=-2}^{v=0}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (0-(-2))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=x-z,\\,w=\\frac{z-1}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-2\\le v\\le 0,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{z-1}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=u.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |u|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{5}u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{0}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}dw \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\cdot [v]_{v=-2}^{v=0}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (0-(-2))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482578 :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{2}={a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=\\frac{p}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{p}{2}+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} &= {(\\frac{p}{2})}^{2}+2(\\frac{p}{2})(\\frac{3}{2})+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\ &= \\frac{{p}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{2}={a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=\\frac{p}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{p}{2}+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} &= {\\left(\\frac{p}{2}\\right)}^{2}+2\\left(\\frac{p}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= \\frac{{p}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482580 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482599 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le e,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)(-1). \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{v=1}^{v=e}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le e,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)(-1). \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{v=1}^{v=e}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482601 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/ec/ec2f6c67eee7f836cc71efec2b17c2276aa8a9d397c632b3c499a46a75f7b95c.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{6}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{8}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi /2}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{9}{\\rho }^{9}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =1} \\\\  & =-(0-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\n