\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{9}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{10}}{10}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=5}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{10}-0)\\cdot (5-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 5,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{9}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{9}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{9}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{10}}{10}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=5}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{10}-0)\\cdot (5-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482615 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+3z,\\,w={z}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3{z}^{2})-3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{w}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [3{w}^{1/3}]_{w=1}^{w=8} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot ({8}^{1/3}-{1}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\