r steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le e\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{e}1\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=-1}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=e} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={y}^{2}-x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le e\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{e}1\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=-1}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=e} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186482617 :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ is $f(x)=-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{96}{595}(4{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-1,\\frac{3}{2}],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 1.$ So, these are critical points as well, but $-1$ is included above.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\pm 1,\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(0) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}-2)=\\frac{192}{595} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(\\frac{3}{2}) & =-\\frac{96}{595}({(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4}-2{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2)=\\frac{138}{595}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{288}{595}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$" "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ is $f(x)=-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{96}{595}(4{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-1,\\frac{3}{2}],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 1.$ So, these are critical points as well, but $-1$ is included above.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\pm 1,\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(0) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}-2)=\\frac{192}{595} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =-\\frac{96}{595}({\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{4}-2{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-2)=\\frac{138}{595}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{288}{595}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186482716 :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=10 x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{20}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{20}}9 \\sin{\\left(10 x \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x &= {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}9\\sin(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= \\frac{9}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sin(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= - \\frac{9 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{10}\\big|_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &= 0-(- \\frac{9}{10}) \\\\ &= \\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we let $u=10 x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\t