>0$ throughout the interval, so the logarithm and substitution are defined." "Let $u=3+\\sinh x$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cosh x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $u=3$. When $x=\\ln 4$,\n\n$$u=3+\\frac{e^{\\ln 4}-e^{-\\ln 4}}{2}\n=3+\\frac{4-\\frac{1}{4}}{2}=\\frac{39}{8}.$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^{\\ln 4}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3+\\sinh x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_3^{\\frac{39}{8}}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\left[\\ln u\\right]_3^{\\frac{39}{8}} \\\\\n&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{39}{8}\\right)-\\ln 3 \\\\\n&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{8}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nHere $u>0$ throughout the interval, so the logarithm and substitution are defined."] [:db/cas 17592186482782 :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{4}{5})" "\\ln \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186482789 :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{13}{8})" "\\ln \\left(\\frac{13}{8}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186482745 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3+\\sinh x$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cosh x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $u=3$. When $x=\\ln 7$,\n\n$$u=3+\\frac{e^{\\ln 7}-e^{-\\ln 7}}{2}\n=3+\\frac{7-\\frac{1}{7}}{2}=\\frac{45}{7}.$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^{\\ln 7}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3+\\sinh x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_3^{\\frac{45}{7}}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\left[\\ln u\\right]_3^{\\frac{45}{7}} \\\\\n&=\\ln(\\frac{45}{7})-\\ln 3 \\\\\n&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{15}{7}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nHere $u>0$ throughout the interval, so the logarithm and substitution are defined." "Let $u=3+\\sinh x$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cosh x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $u=3$. When $x=\\ln 7$,\n\n$$u=3+\\frac{e^{\\ln 7}-e^{-\\ln 7}}{2}\n=3+\\frac{7-\\frac{1}{7}}{2}=\\frac{45}{7}.$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^{\\ln 7}\\frac{\\cosh x}{3+\\sinh x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_3^{\\frac{45}{7}}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\left[\\ln u\\right]_3^{\\frac{45}{7}} \\\\\n&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{45}{7}\\right)-\\ln 3 \\\\\n&=\\ln\\left(\