 "\\frac{10}{2}\\sinh (\\frac{1}{10})" "\\frac{10}{2}\\sinh \\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186491327 :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}\\sinh (\\frac{10}{9})" "\\frac{9}{10}\\sinh \\left(\\frac{10}{9}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186491303 :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\sinh (\\frac{4}{3})" "\\frac{3}{4}\\sinh \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186491308 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{1/2}^{1}\\cosh (\\frac{2x - 1}{4})dx$." "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{1/2}^{1}\\cosh \\left(\\frac{2x - 1}{4}\\right)dx$."] [:db/cas 17592186491308 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{2x-1}{4}$. Then $du=\\frac{2}{4}\\,dx$, so $dx=\\frac{4}{2}\\,du$. The bounds $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1$ become $u=0$ and $u=\\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{2}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\cosh u\\,du = \\frac{4}{2}[\\sinh u]_{0}^{\\frac{1}{4}} = \\frac{4}{2}(\\sinh (\\frac{1}{4})-\\sinh 0) = \\frac{4}{2}\\sinh (\\frac{1}{4})\n$$" "Let $u=\\frac{2x-1}{4}$. Then $du=\\frac{2}{4}\\,dx$, so $dx=\\frac{4}{2}\\,du$. The bounds $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1$ become $u=0$ and $u=\\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{2}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\cosh u\\,du = \\frac{4}{2}[\\sinh u]_{0}^{\\frac{1}{4}} = \\frac{4}{2}(\\sinh \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\sinh 0) = \\frac{4}{2}\\sinh \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491333 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\sinh (\\frac{1}{4})" "\\frac{1}{4}\\sinh \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186491347 :answer/value "\\frac{4}{2}\\sinh (\\frac{1}{4})" "\\frac{4}{2}\\sinh \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186491317 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{1/2}^{1}\\cosh (\\frac{2x - 1}{7})dx$." "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{1/2}^{1}\\cosh \\left(\\frac{2x - 1}{7}\\right)dx$."] [:db/cas 17592186491317 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{2x-1}{7}$. Then $du=\\frac{2}{7}\\,dx$, so $dx=\\frac{7}{2}\\,du$. The bounds $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1$ become $u=0$ and $u=\\frac{1}{7}$.\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{2}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{7}}\\cosh u\\,du = \\frac{7}{2}[\\sinh u]_{0}^{\\frac{1}{7}} = \\frac{7}{2}(\\sinh (\\frac{1}{7})-\\sinh 0) = \\frac{7}{2}\\sinh (\\frac{1}{7})\n$$" "Let $u=\\frac{2x-1}{7}$. Then $du=\\frac{2}{7}\\,dx$, so $dx=\\frac{7}{2}\\,du$. The bounds $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1$ become $u=0$ and $u=\\frac{1}{7}$.\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{2}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{7}}\\cosh u\\,du = \\frac{7}{2}[\\sinh u]_{0}^{\\frac{1}{7}} = \\frac{7}{2}(\\sinh \\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)-\\sinh 0) = \\frac{7}{2}\\sinh \\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491328 :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\sinh (\\frac{1}{7})" "\\frac{7}{2}\\sinh \\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186491344 :answer/value "\\frac{6}{7}\\sinh (\\frac{7}{6})" "\\frac{6}{7}\\sinh \\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186491346 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\sinh (\\frac{1}{7})" "\\frac{1}{7}\\sinh \\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186491382 :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{K}{|5-5x|})" "\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{K}{|5-5x|}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186491403 :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=5-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{5-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int \\frac{1}{5-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-5x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-5x|+C & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-5x|-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-5x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-5x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{6}{5-5x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-5x|).\n$$" "First, let $u=5-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{5-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int \\frac{1}{5-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-5x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-5x|+C & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-5x|-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-5x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-5x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{6}{5-5x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-5x|).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491391 :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{|6x+2|}{K})" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{|6x+2|}{K}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186491394 :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=3x+6.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{3x+6}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{1}{3x+6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+6|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+6|+C & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+6|+\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+6|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+6|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5}{3x+6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+6|).\n$$" "First, let $u=3x+6.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{3x+6}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{1}{3x+6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+6|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+6|+C & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+6|+\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+6|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+6|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5}{3x+6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+6|).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491396 :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=3-4x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-4x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-4x|+C & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-4x|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-4x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3-4x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{3-4x}\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3-4x|).\n$$" "First, let $u=3-4x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-4x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-4x|+C & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-4x|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-4x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3-4x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{3-4x}\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3-4x|).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491983 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=2y-x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1+\\frac{x}{2} & ⟹\\, & 2y-x=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=-1+\\frac{x}{2} & ⟹\\, & 2y-x=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y-x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=x-1 & ⟹\\, & y-x=-1 & ⟹\\, & v=-1 \\\\  & y=x-2 & ⟹\\, & y-x=-2 & ⟹\\, & v=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,-2\\le v\\le -1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e9/e9ad855bcb5133e9a51d990ea25d0e23c8da88c59e15825f3ea1bfc47335dc33.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=2y-x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1+\\frac{x}{2} & ⟹\\, & 2y-x=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=-1+\\frac{x}{2} & ⟹\\, & 2y-x=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y-x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=x-1 & ⟹\\, & y-x=-1 & ⟹\\, & v=-1 \\\\  & y=x-2 & ⟹\\, & y-x=-2 & ⟹\\, & v=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,-2\\le v\\le -1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e9/e9ad855bcb5133e9a51d990ea25d0e23c8da88c59e15825f3ea1bfc47335dc33.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491993 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n2x-y=1,\\,2x-y=3,\\,x-2y=1,\\,x-2y=4\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=2x-y,\\,v=x-2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=2x-y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & 2x-y=3\\,⟹\\,u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x-2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x-2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & x-2y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/87/877dee1177d97f3c0df572e766d47cf31cec941d1770f72f5835433b386d5067.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4+1 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n2x-y=1,\\,2x-y=3,\\,x-2y=1,\\,x-2y=4\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=2x-y,\\,v=x-2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=2x-y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & 2x-y=3\\,⟹\\,u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x-2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x-2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & x-2y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/87/877dee1177d97f3c0df572e766d47cf31cec941d1770f72f5835433b386d5067.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4+1 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491995 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n3x-2y=2,\\,3x-2y=7,\\,2x-3y=1,\\,2x-3y=4\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=3x-2y,\\,v=2x-3y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=3x-2y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3x-2y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & 3x-2y=7\\,⟹\\,u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2x-3y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2x-3y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 2x-3y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 7,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/83/83798f4e941010a24a548d9403b7085fa8e4b8cca8dcb7c0ebc6670d4afbf517.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-9+4 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{2}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (7-2)\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n3x-2y=2,\\,3x-2y=7,\\,2x-3y=1,\\,2x-3y=4\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=3x-2y,\\,v=2x-3y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=3x-2y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3x-2y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & 3x-2y=7\\,⟹\\,u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2x-3y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2x-3y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 2x-3y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 7,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/83/83798f4e941010a24a548d9403b7085fa8e4b8cca8dcb7c0ebc6670d4afbf517.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-9+4 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{2}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (7-2)\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491996 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y={x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=3{x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x={y}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/26/26eaba24af54a40f59f92148c145285ba4097ce765aee8cfc9a6241ff88b9498.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{3{y}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}+1)\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y={x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=3{x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x={y}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/26/26eaba24af54a40f59f92148c145285ba4097ce765aee8cfc9a6241ff88b9498.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{3{y}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}+1)\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492005 :question/worked-solution "Given that $m=\\frac{5}{2}$, we substitute the coordinates of the given point into the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =mx+b \\\\ (7) & =(\\frac{5}{2})(6)+b \\\\ 7 & =15+b \\\\ -8 & =b \\\\ b & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,-8).$" "Given that $m=\\frac{5}{2}$, we substitute the coordinates of the given point into the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =mx+b \\\\ (7) & =\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)(6)+b \\\\ 7 & =15+b \\\\ -8 & =b \\\\ b & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,-8).$"] [:db/cas 17592186492010 :question/worked-solution "Since the line passes through the point $(-6,8)$ and its slope is $\\frac{3}{2}$, we substitute $x=-6$, $y=8,$ and $m=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =mx+b \\\\ (8) & =(\\frac{3}{2})(-6)+b \\\\ 8 & =-\\frac{18}{2}+b \\\\ 8 & =-9+b \\\\ 17 & =b \\\\ b & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is $b=17.$" "Since the line passes through the point $(-6,8)$ and its slope is $\\frac{3}{2}$, we substitute $x=-6$, $y=8,$ and $m=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =mx+b \\\\ (8) & =\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)(-6)+b \\\\ 8 & =-\\frac{18}{2}+b \\\\ 8 & =-9+b \\\\ 17 & =b \\\\ b & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is $b=17.$"] [:db/cas 17592186492015 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+2y,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+2y=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2y)^{2}-x-1=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v-1=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{2}-1 \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c598c1c0d09fb26175cad17106bc4529165da619f35a5882ead0ba133d05c2c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}={(-2)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}-1}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{{u}^{2}-1}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(1-({u}^{2}-1))du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-2\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+2y,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+2y=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2y)^{2}-x-1=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v-1=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{2}-1 \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c598c1c0d09fb26175cad17106bc4529165da619f35a5882ead0ba133d05c2c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}={(-2)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}-1}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{{u}^{2}-1}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(1-({u}^{2}-1))du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-2\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492046 :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}},$ and $b=-{z}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{3}{(-{z}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{2}{(-{z}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{1}{(-{z}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{0}{(-{z}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}})(1)+3(\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{z}^{4}})(-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})({z}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{{z}^{4}}+\\frac{6y{z}^{4}}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+6y{z}^{2}-{z}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the binomial expansion in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3}=-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}.\n$$" "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}},$ and $b=-{z}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{\\left(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}\\right)}^{3}{(-{z}^{2})}^{0}+3{\\left(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}{(-{z}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{\\left(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}\\right)}^{1}{(-{z}^{2})}^{2}+1{\\left(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}\\right)}^{0}{(-{z}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}\\right)(1)+3\\left(\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{z}^{4}}\\right)(-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3\\left(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}\\right)({z}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{{z}^{4}}+\\frac{6y{z}^{4}}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+6y{z}^{2}-{z}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the binomial expansion in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3}=-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492052 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{2t},\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨2{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0},\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${T}^{′}(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{2}{(2{e}^{0}+1)^{2}}⟨{e}^{0}-2{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0},\\,-2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{9}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3}{9}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $r(0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{3}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{2t},\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨2{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0},\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${T}^{′}(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{2}{(2{e}^{0}+1)^{2}}⟨{e}^{0}-2{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0},\\,-2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{9}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3}{9}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $r(0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492055 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(2)=⟨2,2⟩,$ since the point $P(2,2)$ is at the tip of $r(2).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(2)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(2)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2-t}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2-t}{2\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =⟨\\frac{2-2}{2\\sqrt{4(2)-{2}^{2}}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩=⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(2) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(2)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=2$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2)+R(2)N(2) & =⟨2,2⟩+2⟨0,-1⟩=⟨2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(2)=⟨2,2⟩,$ since the point $P(2,2)$ is at the tip of $r(2).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(2)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(2)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}}{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2-t}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2-t}{2\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =⟨\\frac{2-2}{2\\sqrt{4(2)-{2}^{2}}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩=⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(2) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(2)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=2$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2)+R(2)N(2) & =⟨2,2⟩+2⟨0,-1⟩=⟨2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492060 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+(x-y)^{4}=3\\,⟹\\,v+{u}^{4}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=3-{u}^{4} \\\\ v &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm 1 \\\\ v &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,2).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,2\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{4}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/81/81ff220903cbe53cebf5e8f8b704e2a316be6092e1f14e527a7321e8f61bffb8.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{2}^{3-{u}^{4}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[v]_{2}^{3-{u}^{4}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}((3-{u}^{4})-2)\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{4}))\\,du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+(x-y)^{4}=3\\,⟹\\,v+{u}^{4}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=3-{u}^{4} \\\\ v &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm 1 \\\\ v &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,2).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,2\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{4}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/81/81ff220903cbe53cebf5e8f8b704e2a316be6092e1f14e527a7321e8f61bffb8.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{2}^{3-{u}^{4}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[v]_{2}^{3-{u}^{4}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}((3-{u}^{4})-2)\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{4}))\\,du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492063 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y={x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/12/1207d8cdb8afd7c55d712caca043edfe9f0d0395343a6cbd329cf5861ec3bce7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2x}{y} & -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{3{x}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y={x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/12/1207d8cdb8afd7c55d712caca043edfe9f0d0395343a6cbd329cf5861ec3bce7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2x}{y} & -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{3{x}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492223 :answer/value "{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{-6}" "{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-6}"] [:db/cas 17592186492314 :answer/value "{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{27}" "{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{27}"] [:db/cas 17592186492457 :answer/value "{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{12}" "{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{12}"] [:db/cas 17592186492667 :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{27}" "{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{27}"] [:db/cas 17592186492693 :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{12}" "{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{12}"] [:db/cas 17592186492864 :question/problem "${(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\times {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{9}=$" "${\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}\\times {\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{9}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186492864 :question/worked-solution "To multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\times {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{9}={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3+9}={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{12}\n$$" "To multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}\\times {\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{9}={\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3+9}={\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{12}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492847 :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes, and   \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the bulb is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the bulb is  from box $B$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the bulb is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a defective bulb, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since a box is chosen at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $2$ defective bulbs out of $15$  in box $A$ and  $1$ defective bulb out of $10$ in box $B,$  we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=\\frac{2}{15},\\,P(D|B)=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected bulb is defective is $\\frac{2}{15},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =(\\frac{2}{15})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(D|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =\\frac{2}{45}+\\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{1}{3}P(D|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}P(D|C) & =\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ P(D|C) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected bulb from box $C$ is defective is $\\frac{1}{6}.$" "Let $D$ be the event of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes, and   \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the bulb is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the bulb is  from box $B$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the bulb is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a defective bulb, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since a box is chosen at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $2$ defective bulbs out of $15$  in box $A$ and  $1$ defective bulb out of $10$ in box $B,$  we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=\\frac{2}{15},\\,P(D|B)=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected bulb is defective is $\\frac{2}{15},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =\\left(\\frac{2}{15}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+P(D|C)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =\\frac{2}{45}+\\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{1}{3}P(D|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}P(D|C) & =\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ P(D|C) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected bulb from box $C$ is defective is $\\frac{1}{6}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186492897 :question/worked-solution "The exact amount of alcohol is ${(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2}$ liters.\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{3}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\times 8}{3\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{64}{9}.\n$$" "The exact amount of alcohol is ${\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}^{2}$ liters.\n\nWriting ${\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}^{2} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{3}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\times 8}{3\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{64}{9}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492926 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,3)$ and passes through the point $(-2,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,\\frac{5}{2}),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2+1)^{2} & =4p(\\frac{5}{2}-3) \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =4p(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 1 & =-2p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-2(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-2(0-3) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-1-\\sqrt{6}$ and $-1+\\sqrt{6}.$" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,3)$ and passes through the point $(-2,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,\\frac{5}{2}),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2+1)^{2} & =4p(\\frac{5}{2}-3) \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =4p\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ 1 & =-2p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-2(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-2(0-3) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-1-\\sqrt{6}$ and $-1+\\sqrt{6}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186492928 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,-1)$ and passes through the point $(0,\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,\\frac{5}{4}),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{5}{4}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-3)^{2} & =4p(\\frac{5}{4}+1) \\\\ 9 & =4p(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $1$  and $5.$" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,-1)$ and passes through the point $(0,\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,\\frac{5}{4}),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{5}{4}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-3)^{2} & =4p(\\frac{5}{4}+1) \\\\ 9 & =4p\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $1$  and $5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186493316 :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(10 x^{2} + 5 x)=20 x + 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1)=25=25,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(25)}=- \\frac{1}{25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(15)&=- \\frac{1}{25}(x-(1))\\\\y&=- \\frac{1}{25}x-(- \\frac{1}{25})+(15)\\\\y&=- \\frac{1}{25}x+\\frac{376}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{25}x+\\frac{376}{25}&=0\\\\x&=376.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(376,0).$" "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(10 x^{2} + 5 x)=20 x + 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1)=25=25,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(25)}=- \\frac{1}{25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(15)&=- \\frac{1}{25}(x-(1))\\\\y&=- \\frac{1}{25}x-\\left(- \\frac{1}{25}\\right)+(15)\\\\y&=- \\frac{1}{25}x+\\frac{376}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{25}x+\\frac{376}{25}&=0\\\\x&=376.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(376,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186493318 :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(\\frac{20}{x}+10)\\\\&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(10 + \\frac{20}{x})\\\\&=-20x^{-2}\\\\&=- \\frac{20}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1)=-20=-20,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-20)}=\\frac{1}{20}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(30)&=\\frac{1}{20}(x-(1))\\\\y&=\\frac{1}{20}x-(\\frac{1}{20})+(30)\\\\y&=\\frac{1}{20}x+\\frac{599}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20}x+\\frac{599}{20}&=0\\\\x&=-599.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the  $x$-axis at $(-599,0).$" "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(\\frac{20}{x}+10)\\\\&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(10 + \\frac{20}{x})\\\\&=-20x^{-2}\\\\&=- \\frac{20}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1)=-20=-20,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-20)}=\\frac{1}{20}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(30)&=\\frac{1}{20}(x-(1))\\\\y&=\\frac{1}{20}x-\\left(\\frac{1}{20}\\right)+(30)\\\\y&=\\frac{1}{20}x+\\frac{599}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20}x+\\frac{599}{20}&=0\\\\x&=-599.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the  $x$-axis at $(-599,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186493343 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4y,\\,y(- \\frac{3}{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4y,\\,y\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)=-1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186493343 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(- \\frac{3}{2})=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(- \\frac{3}{2})=-1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{4x} \\\\ -1 & =A{e}^{4\\cdot (- \\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ -1 & =A{e}^{-6} \\\\ \\frac{-1}{{e}^{-6}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{-1}{{e}^{-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're given that $y=A{e}^{4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)=-1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{4x} \\\\ -1 & =A{e}^{4\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\ -1 & =A{e}^{-6} \\\\ \\frac{-1}{{e}^{-6}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{-1}{{e}^{-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493353 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4y,\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4y,\\,y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186493353 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{3}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =A{e}^{-2} \\\\ \\frac{\\frac{1}{3}}{{e}^{-2}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}}{{e}^{-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{3}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =A{e}^{-2} \\\\ \\frac{\\frac{1}{3}}{{e}^{-2}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}}{{e}^{-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493355 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y,\\qquad y(- \\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y,\\qquad y\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186493355 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(- \\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(- \\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{3}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =A{e}^{3\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =A{e}^{- \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\frac{1}{3}}{{e}^{- \\frac{3}{2}}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}}{{e}^{- \\frac{3}{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{3}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =A{e}^{3\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =A{e}^{- \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\frac{1}{3}}{{e}^{- \\frac{3}{2}}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}}{{e}^{- \\frac{3}{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493364 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y,\\,y(\\frac{3}{2})=1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y,\\,y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186493364 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(\\frac{3}{2})=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(\\frac{3}{2})=1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{3x} \\\\ 1 & =A{e}^{3\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ 1 & =A{e}^{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{\\frac{9}{2}}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\frac{9}{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{3x} \\\\ 1 & =A{e}^{3\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ 1 & =A{e}^{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{\\frac{9}{2}}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\frac{9}{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493365 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4y,\\,y(\\frac{3}{2})=3.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4y,\\,y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=3.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186493365 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(\\frac{3}{2})=3.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(\\frac{3}{2})=3$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{-6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{{e}^{-6}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{3}{{e}^{-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=3.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=3$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{-6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{{e}^{-6}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{3}{{e}^{-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493892 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $125$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{125}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that the numbers $125$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{125}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493898 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the weekly exercise time of a randomly selected person. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{30}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<220).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =25.248 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{25.248}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈252.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<220)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<220) & =P(Z<\\frac{220-252.48}{30}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.08) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.08) \\\\  & =0.1401 \\\\  & ≈14.01%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $14.01%$ of the people exercise less than $220$ minutes per week." "Let $X$ represent the weekly exercise time of a randomly selected person. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{30}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<220).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}\\right) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =25.248 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{25.248}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈252.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<220)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<220) & =P(Z<\\frac{220-252.48}{30}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.08) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.08) \\\\  & =0.1401 \\\\  & ≈14.01%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $14.01%$ of the people exercise less than $220$ minutes per week."] [:db/cas 17592186493899 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the grade obtained by a student. Then we have $X∼N(81,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>91)=0.05.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =1-P(X>91) \\\\  & =1-0.05 \\\\  & =0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{91-81}{\\sigma }) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{10}{\\sigma }) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{10}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)≈1.6449.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{\\sigma } & =1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.16449 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.16449} \\\\  & ≈6.0794.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(X\\le 70) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{70-81}{6.0794}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.81) \\\\  & =0.0351 \\\\  & ≈3.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.5%$ of the students scored lower than $70.$" "Let $X$ represent the grade obtained by a student. Then we have $X∼N(81,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>91)=0.05.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =1-P(X>91) \\\\  & =1-0.05 \\\\  & =0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{91-81}{\\sigma }) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{10}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{10}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)≈1.6449.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{\\sigma } & =1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.16449 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.16449} \\\\  & ≈6.0794.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(X\\le 70) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{70-81}{6.0794}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.81) \\\\  & =0.0351 \\\\  & ≈3.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.5%$ of the students scored lower than $70.$"] [:db/cas 17592186493917 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the output voltage of a randomly selected AC adapter. Then we have $X∼N(19,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>19.3).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>20)=0.01.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =1-P(X>20) \\\\  & =1-0.01 \\\\  & =0.99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =0.99 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-19}{\\sigma }) & =0.99 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1}{\\sigma }) & =0.99 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.99)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.99)≈2.3263.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =2.3263 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{2.32639} \\\\  & ≈0.42985.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>19.3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>19.3) & =1-P(X\\le 19.3) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{19.3-19}{0.42985}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le 0.70) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.70) \\\\  & =1-0.7580 \\\\  & ≈24.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $24.2%$ of AC adapters have a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts." "Let $X$ represent the output voltage of a randomly selected AC adapter. Then we have $X∼N(19,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>19.3).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>20)=0.01.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =1-P(X>20) \\\\  & =1-0.01 \\\\  & =0.99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =0.99 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-19}{\\sigma }) & =0.99 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.99 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.99)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.99)≈2.3263.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =2.3263 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{2.32639} \\\\  & ≈0.42985.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>19.3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>19.3) & =1-P(X\\le 19.3) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{19.3-19}{0.42985}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le 0.70) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.70) \\\\  & =1-0.7580 \\\\  & ≈24.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $24.2%$ of AC adapters have a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts."] [:db/cas 17592186493919 :question/worked-solution "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So, we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3/2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{\\,3}-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So, we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3/2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{\\,3}-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493923 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+2y+3z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y-z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 2 &=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 3 &=-\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=∓\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})-z & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})-z & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $-\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its   maximum value is $\\sqrt{5}.$" "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+2y+3z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y-z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 2 &=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 3 &=-\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=∓\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)-z & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)-z & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $-\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its   maximum value is $\\sqrt{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186493945 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $27$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{27}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that the numbers $27$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{27}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493956 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2y}{x})}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{-v/2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1/4}^{2}]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{4}]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\cdot {[-2{e}^{-v/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot ({e}^{-2}-{e}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{2y}{x}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{-v/2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1/4}^{2}]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{4}]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\cdot {[-2{e}^{-v/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot ({e}^{-2}-{e}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493960 :question/problem "Expressed as an integer, $(-4)÷(\\frac{2}{5})\\times 6=${{field-1}}" "Expressed as an integer, $(-4)÷\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\times 6=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186493960 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)÷\\frac{2}{5}\\times 6 &= ((-4)÷\\frac{2}{5})\\times 6 \\\\ &= (\\frac{-4}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{2})\\times 6 \\\\ &= (\\frac{(-4)\\times 5}{1\\times 2})\\times 6 \\\\ &= (-\\frac{20}{2})\\times 6 \\\\ &= (-10)\\times 6 \\\\ &= -60\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)÷\\frac{2}{5}\\times 6 &= ((-4)÷\\frac{2}{5})\\times 6 \\\\ &= (\\frac{-4}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{2})\\times 6 \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{(-4)\\times 5}{1\\times 2}\\right)\\times 6 \\\\ &= \\left(-\\frac{20}{2}\\right)\\times 6 \\\\ &= (-10)\\times 6 \\\\ &= -60\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493961 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493962 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the length of a randomly selected newborn. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{6}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.6.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.6 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.6 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.3 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.3 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.8 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6}) & =0.8 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6}) & =0.8 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{6} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.8)≈0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =5.0496 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{5.0496}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈50.50\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $X$ represent the length of a randomly selected newborn. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{6}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.6.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.6 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.6 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.3 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.3 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.8 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6}) & =0.8 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6}\\right) & =0.8 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{6} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.8)≈0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =5.0496 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{5.0496}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈50.50\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493970 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the package weight (in kilograms). Then we have $X∼N(2.5,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 2.3)=0.20.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.3) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.3-2.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ -\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)=-0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & =-0.8416 \\\\ \\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & =0.8416 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.2}{0.8416} \\\\  & ≈0.24\\,\\text{kg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $X$ represent the package weight (in kilograms). Then we have $X∼N(2.5,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 2.3)=0.20.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.3) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.3-2.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ\\left(-\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.20 \\\\ -\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)=-0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & =-0.8416 \\\\ \\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & =0.8416 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.2}{0.8416} \\\\  & ≈0.24\\,\\text{kg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493973 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=x+2y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |-1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=x+2y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |-1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493975 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the amount of coffee in a randomly selected cup. Then we have $X∼N(10,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<8.5).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nWe know that $P(X<9.1)=0.15.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9.1) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{9.1-10}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ \\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.15)=-1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & =-1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{0.9}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.9}{1.0364} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈0.8684.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<8.5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<8.5) & =P(X\\le 8.5) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{8.5-10}{0.8684}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.73) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.73) \\\\  & =0.0418 \\\\  & ≈4.18%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $4.18%$ of coffee cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee." "Let $X$ represent the amount of coffee in a randomly selected cup. Then we have $X∼N(10,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<8.5).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nWe know that $P(X<9.1)=0.15.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9.1) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{9.1-10}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.15 \\\\ \\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.15)=-1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & =-1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{0.9}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.9}{1.0364} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈0.8684.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<8.5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<8.5) & =P(X\\le 8.5) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{8.5-10}{0.8684}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.73) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.73) \\\\  & =0.0418 \\\\  & ≈4.18%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $4.18%$ of coffee cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee."] [:db/cas 17592186495050 :question/problem "Given that $y={(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{(2x)}^{-x}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" "Given that $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)}^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{(2x)}^{-x}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186495050 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x}))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})+x\\cdot ((2x)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{(2x)^{2}})) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})-1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-{(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1) \\\\  & =-{(2x)}^{-x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)+x\\cdot ((2x)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{(2x)^{2}}\\right)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)-1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1) \\\\  & =-{(2x)}^{-x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186495056 :question/worked-solution "We replace $x$ with $-5$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{x}+\\frac{2}{4x} &= \\frac{8}{(-5)}+\\frac{2}{4\\cdot (-5)} \\\\ &= -\\frac{8}{5}+(-\\frac{2}{20}) \\\\ &= -\\frac{8}{5}-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ &= -\\frac{16}{10}-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ &= \\frac{-16-1}{10} \\\\ &= -\\frac{17}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We replace $x$ with $-5$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{x}+\\frac{2}{4x} &= \\frac{8}{(-5)}+\\frac{2}{4\\cdot (-5)} \\\\ &= -\\frac{8}{5}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{20}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\frac{8}{5}-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ &= -\\frac{16}{10}-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ &= \\frac{-16-1}{10} \\\\ &= -\\frac{17}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186495059 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y+2x.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|-\\frac{1}{4}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|]_{2}^{6}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y+2x.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|-\\frac{1}{4}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|]_{2}^{6}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186495100 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=xy\\,\\text{and}\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2y}{x})}=\\frac{1}{2v}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{1}{2v}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)[{\\int }_{1}^{5}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u){[\\frac{1}{2}\\,{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{5}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\cdot 12\\,du \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\,du \\\\  & =6{[-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi u)]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=xy\\,\\text{and}\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{2y}{x}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{2v}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{1}{2v}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)[{\\int }_{1}^{5}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u){[\\frac{1}{2}\\,{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{5}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\cdot 12\\,du \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\,du \\\\  & =6{[-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi u)]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186495170 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496226 :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the second equation is already solved for $u:$\n\n$$\nu=-v-2\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $u$ with $-v-2$ in the first equation and solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-6v & =-3 \\\\ 4(-v-2)-6v & =-3 \\\\ -4v-8-6v & =-3 \\\\ -10v & =5 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into $u=-v-2,$ then we can find the value of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-v-2 \\\\ u & =-(-\\frac{1}{2})-2 \\\\ u & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$" "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the second equation is already solved for $u:$\n\n$$\nu=-v-2\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $u$ with $-v-2$ in the first equation and solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-6v & =-3 \\\\ 4(-v-2)-6v & =-3 \\\\ -4v-8-6v & =-3 \\\\ -10v & =5 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into $u=-v-2,$ then we can find the value of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-v-2 \\\\ u & =-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-2 \\\\ u & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186496243 :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3\\pi $ is $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{3\\pi +2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\pi +2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\pi +2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+k\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only three lie within the interval $[0,3\\pi ].$ These are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3\\pi .$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2},3\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{1}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+1}{3\\pi +2}=0 \\\\ f(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f(3\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{1}{3\\pi +2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$" "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3\\pi $ is $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{3\\pi +2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\pi +2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\pi +2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+k\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only three lie within the interval $[0,3\\pi ].$ These are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3\\pi .$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2},3\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{1}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+1}{3\\pi +2}=0 \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f(3\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{1}{3\\pi +2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=f\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496249 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3k-2}{6} & =-(k+2) \\\\ 6\\cdot (\\frac{3k-2}{6}) & =6\\cdot (-(k+2)) \\\\ \\cancel{6}\\cdot (\\frac{3k-2}{\\cancel{6}}) & =-6(k+2) \\\\ 3k-2 & =-6k-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k-2 & =-6k-12 \\\\ 3k-2+6k & =-6k-12+6k \\\\ 9k-2 & =-12 \\\\ 9k-2+2 & =-12+2 \\\\ 9k & =-10 \\\\ \\frac{9k}{9} & =\\frac{-10}{9} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{10}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3k-2}{6} & =-(k+2) \\\\ 6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3k-2}{6}\\right) & =6\\cdot (-(k+2)) \\\\ \\cancel{6}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3k-2}{\\cancel{6}}\\right) & =-6(k+2) \\\\ 3k-2 & =-6k-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k-2 & =-6k-12 \\\\ 3k-2+6k & =-6k-12+6k \\\\ 9k-2 & =-12 \\\\ 9k-2+2 & =-12+2 \\\\ 9k & =-10 \\\\ \\frac{9k}{9} & =\\frac{-10}{9} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{10}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496261 :question/problem "The work applied to an object is the result of multiplying the force by the distance. Given that the force is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}),$ determine the unit of work in terms of kilograms, meters and seconds." "The work applied to an object is the result of multiplying the force by the distance. Given that the force is measured in Newtons $\\left(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\right),$ determine the unit of work in terms of kilograms, meters and seconds."] [:db/cas 17592186496261 :question/worked-solution "The force is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})$ and distance is measured in meters ($\\text{m}$). Since the work is the multiplication of force by distance, we must multiply force units by distance units. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{m}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" "The force is measured in Newtons $\\left(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\right)$ and distance is measured in meters ($\\text{m}$). Since the work is the multiplication of force by distance, we must multiply force units by distance units. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{m}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496264 :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+6{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ (-t)^{2}+2(t)^{2}+6(t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$" "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+6{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ (-t)^{2}+2(t)^{2}+6(t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186496271 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2.$ We must make sure that all of the terms on the right-hand side are multiplied by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3h-4}{2} & =2-h \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{3h-4}{2}) & =2\\cdot (2-h) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3h-4}{\\cancel{2}}) & =2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot h \\\\ 3h-4 & =4-2h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3h-4 & =4-2h \\\\ 3h-4+2h & =4-2h+2h \\\\ 5h-4 & =4 \\\\ 5h-4+4 & =4+4 \\\\ 5h & =8 \\\\ \\frac{5h}{5} & =\\frac{8}{5} \\\\ h & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2.$ We must make sure that all of the terms on the right-hand side are multiplied by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3h-4}{2} & =2-h \\\\ 2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3h-4}{2}\\right) & =2\\cdot (2-h) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3h-4}{\\cancel{2}}\\right) & =2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot h \\\\ 3h-4 & =4-2h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3h-4 & =4-2h \\\\ 3h-4+2h & =4-2h+2h \\\\ 5h-4 & =4 \\\\ 5h-4+4 & =4+4 \\\\ 5h & =8 \\\\ \\frac{5h}{5} & =\\frac{8}{5} \\\\ h & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496277 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $7$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-x}{3} & =7x-15 \\\\ 3(\\frac{5-x}{3}) & =3(7x-15) \\\\ \\cancel{3}(\\frac{5-x}{\\cancel{3}}) & =21x-45 \\\\ 5-x & =21x-45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-x & =21x-45 \\\\ 5-x+x & =21x-45+x \\\\ 5 & =22x-45 \\\\ 5+45 & =22x-45+45 \\\\ 50 & =22x \\\\ \\frac{50}{22} & =\\frac{22x}{22} \\\\ \\frac{25}{11} & =\\frac{\\cancel{22}x}{\\cancel{22}} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $7$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-x}{3} & =7x-15 \\\\ 3\\left(\\frac{5-x}{3}\\right) & =3(7x-15) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\left(\\frac{5-x}{\\cancel{3}}\\right) & =21x-45 \\\\ 5-x & =21x-45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-x & =21x-45 \\\\ 5-x+x & =21x-45+x \\\\ 5 & =22x-45 \\\\ 5+45 & =22x-45+45 \\\\ 50 & =22x \\\\ \\frac{50}{22} & =\\frac{22x}{22} \\\\ \\frac{25}{11} & =\\frac{\\cancel{22}x}{\\cancel{22}} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496280 :question/worked-solution "We *measure the change* in the speed, as *we change* the time. Consequently, any appropriate unit must take the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{unit of speed}}{\\text{unit of time}}.\n$$\n\nIn terms of meters and seconds, the unit for speed is $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}},$ and the unit for time is $\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the appropriate unit for the rate of change of speed is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}})}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nor, meters per square second." "We *measure the change* in the speed, as *we change* the time. Consequently, any appropriate unit must take the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{unit of speed}}{\\text{unit of time}}.\n$$\n\nIn terms of meters and seconds, the unit for speed is $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}},$ and the unit for time is $\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the appropriate unit for the rate of change of speed is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\\right)}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nor, meters per square second."] [:db/cas 17592186496282 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+2z=-3\\,⟹\\,z=-\\frac{x+3}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the hyperboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-2{(-\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1.$" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+2z=-3\\,⟹\\,z=-\\frac{x+3}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the hyperboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-2{\\left(-\\frac{x+3}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496290 :question/worked-solution "To find the unit of the force, we substitute the given units of work and distance into the formula and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\frac{W}{d} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{m}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{m}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot \\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of the force is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" "To find the unit of the force, we substitute the given units of work and distance into the formula and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\frac{W}{d} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\right)}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{m}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot \\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of the force is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496331 :question/problem "The work $W$ applied to a body is related to force $F$ and distance $d$ by the equation $W=F\\cdot d.$ If $F$ is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})$ and $d$ is measured in meters, what is an appropriate measurement unit for work?" "The work $W$ applied to a body is related to force $F$ and distance $d$ by the equation $W=F\\cdot d.$ If $F$ is measured in Newtons $\\left(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\right)$ and $d$ is measured in meters, what is an appropriate measurement unit for work?"] [:db/cas 17592186496333 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $9$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2t & =\\frac{4-6t}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4-6t}{9} & =-2t \\\\ 9(\\frac{4-6t}{9}) & =9(-2t) \\\\ \\cancel{9}(\\frac{4-6t}{\\cancel{9}}) & =-18t \\\\ 4-6t & =-18t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-6t & =-18t \\\\ 4-6t+6t & =-18t+6t \\\\ 4 & =-12t \\\\ -12t & =4 \\\\ \\frac{-12t}{-12} & =\\frac{4}{-12} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-12}t}{\\cancel{-12}} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $9$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2t & =\\frac{4-6t}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4-6t}{9} & =-2t \\\\ 9\\left(\\frac{4-6t}{9}\\right) & =9(-2t) \\\\ \\cancel{9}\\left(\\frac{4-6t}{\\cancel{9}}\\right) & =-18t \\\\ 4-6t & =-18t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-6t & =-18t \\\\ 4-6t+6t & =-18t+6t \\\\ 4 & =-12t \\\\ -12t & =4 \\\\ \\frac{-12t}{-12} & =\\frac{4}{-12} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-12}t}{\\cancel{-12}} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496407 :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- \\frac{9 \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =- \\frac{9}{2}\\int \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =- \\frac{9}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\left(x + 2\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right)+C \\\\ y & =- \\frac{3 \\left(x + 2\\right)^{3}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/f0/f0abc54ee55826263306f014480e9a4d36c4d0d3fd10b4c51757d03f38d00ae6.png)" "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- \\frac{9 \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =- \\frac{9}{2}\\int \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =- \\frac{9}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\left(x + 2\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right)+C \\\\ y & =- \\frac{3 \\left(x + 2\\right)^{3}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/f0/f0abc54ee55826263306f014480e9a4d36c4d0d3fd10b4c51757d03f38d00ae6.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186496418 :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{9 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{2}\\int \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\left(x - 2\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right)+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{3 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{3}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/12/1235c9c0d6913436949a82342c89149bcd494498e6bbfd10c69aecb6b471c5c6.png)" "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{9 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{2}\\int \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\left(x - 2\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right)+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{3 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{3}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/12/1235c9c0d6913436949a82342c89149bcd494498e6bbfd10c69aecb6b471c5c6.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186496429 :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}}{8})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\left(- 4 e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}\\right)+C \\\\ y & =- \\frac{e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/25/258e0e2dea305737216c1a4cbc8f448d93055b1c97eb8b8e73805b7fb8425c73.png)" "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}}{8}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\left(- 4 e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}\\right)+C \\\\ y & =- \\frac{e^{- \\frac{x}{4}}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/25/258e0e2dea305737216c1a4cbc8f448d93055b1c97eb8b8e73805b7fb8425c73.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186496432 :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4 e^{- \\frac{2 x}{3}}}{9})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{9}\\int e^{- \\frac{2 x}{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}\\left(- \\frac{3 e^{- \\frac{2 x}{3}}}{2}\\right)+C \\\\ y & =- \\frac{2 e^{- \\frac{2 x}{3}}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/ae/ae0e43d8605937216fd9b02d433085e87cf87e6f3bc66f3b8d0d20a86aada3d2.png)" "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{4 e^{- \\frac{2 x}{3}}}{9}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{9}\\int e^{- \\frac{2 x}{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}\\left(- \\frac{3 e^{- \\frac{2 x}{3}}}{2}\\right)+C \\\\ y & =- \\frac{2 e^{- \\frac{2 x}{3}}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/ae/ae0e43d8605937216fd9b02d433085e87cf87e6f3bc66f3b8d0d20a86aada3d2.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186496445 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 3 \\cdot 2^{x} + 5 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- 3 \\cdot 2^{x} + 5 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((5)\\int \\left(x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-3)\\int \\left(2^{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((2)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((5)\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)) + ((-3)\\left(\\frac{2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)) + ((2)\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right))+C \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}} + C + \\frac{5 x^{2}}{2} - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}} + C + \\frac{5 x^{2}}{2} - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =((\\frac{5}{2})\\int \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((- \\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}})\\int \\left(2^{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-4)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =((\\frac{5}{2})\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)) + ((- \\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}})\\left(\\frac{2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)) + ((-4)\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{2}} + C x + D + \\frac{5 x^{3}}{6} - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 3 \\cdot 2^{x} + 5 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- 3 \\cdot 2^{x} + 5 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((5)\\int \\left(x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-3)\\int \\left(2^{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((2)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((5)\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)) + ((-3)\\left(\\frac{2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)) + ((2)\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right))+C \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}} + C + \\frac{5 x^{2}}{2} - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}} + C + \\frac{5 x^{2}}{2} - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + (\\left(- \\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)\\int \\left(2^{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-4)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)) + (\\left(- \\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)) + ((-4)\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{2}} + C x + D + \\frac{5 x^{3}}{6} - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496457 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- x + 7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 8 e^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- x + 7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 8 e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-1)\\int \\left(x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-8)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((7)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((-1)\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)) + ((-8)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)) + ((7)\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right))+C \\\\  & =C - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{7 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2} + 4 e^{- 2 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{7 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2} + 4 e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =((- \\frac{1}{2})\\int \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((4)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((\\frac{7}{2})\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =((- \\frac{1}{2})\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)) + ((4)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)) + ((\\frac{7}{2})\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{x^{3}}{6} - \\frac{7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4} - 2 e^{- 2 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- x + 7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 8 e^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- x + 7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 8 e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-1)\\int \\left(x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-8)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((7)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((-1)\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)) + ((-8)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)) + ((7)\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right))+C \\\\  & =C - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{7 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2} + 4 e^{- 2 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{7 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2} + 4 e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =(\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((4)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + (\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)) + ((4)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)) + (\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{x^{3}}{6} - \\frac{7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4} - 2 e^{- 2 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496459 :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-1)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((-1)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right))+C \\\\  & =C + \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C + \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =((\\frac{1}{2})\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =((\\frac{1}{2})\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $