= \\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\ &= {x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503879 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}={x}^{5/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{(\\frac{5}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{(\\frac{7}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}={x}^{5/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{(\\frac{5}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503882 :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{(\\frac{5}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}