orname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186504092 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186504155 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186504322 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186504327 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{\\pi n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\pi n}]" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{k}{\\pi n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\pi n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186504688 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi .$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi -0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi .$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi -0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f\\left(\\frac{\\pi k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504138 :answer/value "\\cos(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})" "\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186504609 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi /6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi /6-0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(\\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi /6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi /6-0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi i}{6n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504445 :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186504616 :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ -m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504611 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{cx+4}, & x\\ne -1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{27}, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $c?$" "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{cx+4}, & x\\ne -1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{27}, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186504611 :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{cx+4}={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ 4-c & =3 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{cx+4}={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{4-c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{4-c} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{4-c} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ 4-c & =3 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504613 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ directly into the limit, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ) & =12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})+\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ directly into the limit, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ) & =12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)+\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504614 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if  $-\\frac{3}{x+3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{3}{x+3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48610f07da2103a0e1416318c1c70da426d8c845f0870387836098287d83efb8.png)" "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if  $-\\frac{3}{x+3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{3}{x+3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}\\left(-\\frac{3}{x+3}\\right) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}\\left(-\\frac{3}{x+3}\\right) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48610f07da2103a0e1416318c1c70da426d8c845f0870387836098287d83efb8.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186504625 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504629 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}))=$" "${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right))=$"] [:db/cas 17592186504629 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}):$   \n\n![](images/4a/4abceba56f404b0b39fbde4b1f7da0ec36e69a496c4ef52d3bebfbcb8c7d3380.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$    \n\nNow, substituting $x=3$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right):$   \n\n![](images/4a/4abceba56f404b0b39fbde4b1f7da0ec36e69a496c4ef52d3bebfbcb8c7d3380.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$    \n\nNow, substituting $x=3$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504631 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{x-3})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/13/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)" "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x-3}\\right)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/13/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186504635 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2(-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186504637 :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))+({x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=e/3} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)}) \\\\  & =(1)^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0+\\frac{e}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))+({x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=e/3} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))^{(e/3)^{2}}(2\\left(\\frac{e}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{e}{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)}) \\\\  & =(1)^{(e/3)^{2}}(2\\left(\\frac{e}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{e}{3}\\right)}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0+\\frac{e}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504644 :question/worked-solution "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\n \n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\n \n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504651 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-2}, & x\\ne 4, \\\\ c, & x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4,$ what is the value of $c?$" "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x-2}, & x\\ne 4, \\\\ c, & x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4,$ what is the value of $c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186504651 :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-2}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x-2}={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504655 :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$" "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}\\right){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"] [:db/cas 17592186504667 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either ${x}^{2}-1$ or $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of ${x}^{2}-1.$ Since ${x}^{2}-1$ is a polynomial, therefore it cannot grow without bound due to a vertical asymptote. So this function cannot have an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{x-1}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>1$, we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\frac{2}{x-1})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the only point where the function $y=f(x)$ has an infinite discontinuity is  $x=1.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cba4d1e7cab2340e24d176cee3e03c6a103cf0c69fbf15209c5f4b92a4993e2.png)" "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either ${x}^{2}-1$ or $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of ${x}^{2}-1.$ Since ${x}^{2}-1$ is a polynomial, therefore it cannot grow without bound due to a vertical asymptote. So this function cannot have an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{x-1}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>1$, we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{2}{x-1}\\right)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the only point where the function $y=f(x)$ has an infinite discontinuity is  $x=1.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cba4d1e7cab2340e24d176cee3e03c6a103cf0c69fbf15209c5f4b92a4993e2.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186504674 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\frac{1}{5-x}$ or $\\frac{1}{8-x}$ grow without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{5-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n5-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x<5,$ which excludes $x=5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{5-x})=+\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{8-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n8-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $5\\le x<8$ and $x>8,$ which excludes $x=8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=\\infty \\,,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=8$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the function $y=f(x)$ has infinite discontinuities at \n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7773030c42d9e26a737ad1ed0208900101ed291189c7a1bc1430a808659ba33.png)" "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\frac{1}{5-x}$ or $\\frac{1}{8-x}$ grow without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{5-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n5-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x<5,$ which excludes $x=5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{5-x}\\right)=+\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{8-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n8-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $5\\le x<8$ and $x>8,$ which excludes $x=8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{8-x}\\right)=\\infty \\,,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{8-x}\\right)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=8$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the function $y=f(x)$ has infinite discontinuities at \n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7773030c42d9e26a737ad1ed0208900101ed291189c7a1bc1430a808659ba33.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186504684 :question/worked-solution "![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504686 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x):$ \n\n![](images/87/873a6fb793d2ae68eab172132e467a48a43ab84733ad92155fe6cc012f516382.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=6\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})=3\\pi .\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x):$ \n\n![](images/87/873a6fb793d2ae68eab172132e467a48a43ab84733ad92155fe6cc012f516382.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=3\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504700 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+2)^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}+2\\,)}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+2)^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f\\left(\\frac{2i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}+2\\,)}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504703 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=5e{x}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e{x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & ={x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=5e{x}^{1/x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5e{x}^{1/x}[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)] \\\\  & =5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=5e{x}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e{x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & ={x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=5e{x}^{1/x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5e{x}^{1/x}[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)] \\\\  & =5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504708 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $-\\frac{1}{x+8}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{1}{x+8}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-8.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{-}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{+}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-8$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed2cae2a50cb15f86ead5831990366db2048c100d71dc9d1b681b890da38844f.png)" "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $-\\frac{1}{x+8}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{1}{x+8}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-8.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{-}}\\left(-\\frac{1}{x+8}\\right) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{+}}\\left(-\\frac{1}{x+8}\\right) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-8$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed2cae2a50cb15f86ead5831990366db2048c100d71dc9d1b681b890da38844f.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186504709 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186504753 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b)=λ(\\frac{1}{2})a+λ(4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-3}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-24}{4} = -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-3a - 24b=-6(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative." "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-3}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-24}{4} = -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-3a - 24b=-6(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."] [:db/cas 17592186504768 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{4}a - 3b)=λ(\\frac{1}{4})a+λ(-3)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{4}} = -4 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{12}{-3} = -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a + 12b=-4(\\frac{1}{4}a - 3b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-4\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative." "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{4}a - 3b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)a+λ(-3)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{4}} = -4 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{12}{-3} = -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a + 12b=-4(\\frac{1}{4}a - 3b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-4\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."] [:db/cas 17592186504832 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 3b)=λ(\\frac{1}{2})a+λ(3)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -2 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-6}{3} = -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a - 6b=-2(\\frac{1}{2}a + 3b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-2\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative." "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 3b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(3)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -2 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-6}{3} = -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a - 6b=-2(\\frac{1}{2}a + 3b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-2\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."] [:db/cas 17592186504838 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b)=λ(\\frac{1}{2})a+λ(4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{3}{\\frac{1}{2}} = 6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n3a + 24b=6(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are the same because the factor is positive." "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{3}{\\frac{1}{2}} = 6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n3a + 24b=6(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are the same because the factor is positive."] [:db/cas 17592186504839 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + b)=λ(\\frac{1}{2})a+λ(1)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -2 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-2}{1} = -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a - 2b=-2(\\frac{1}{2}a + b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-2\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative." "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(1)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -2 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-2}{1} = -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a - 2b=-2(\\frac{1}{2}a + b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-2\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."] [:db/cas 17592186505110 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2-y}{2+y})" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-y}{2+y}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186505280 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2y-1}{2y+1})" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2y-1}{2y+1}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186505527 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y-2}{y+2})" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-2}{y+2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186505533 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y+2}{y-2})" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{y+2}{y-2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186505564 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+y}{2-y})" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2+y}{2-y}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186505778 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{y}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{y}{2})}{1-(-\\frac{y}{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{y}{2}}{1+\\frac{y}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{2-y}{2}}{\\frac{2+y}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2-y}{2+y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+\\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}{1-\\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1-\\frac{y}{2}}{1+\\frac{y}{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\frac{2-y}{2}}{\\frac{2+y}{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-y}{2+y}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505167 :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-x}{1+x})}" "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1-x}{1+x}\\right)}"] [:db/cas 17592186505304 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186505327 :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right)}"] [:db/cas 17592186505501 :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right)}"] [:db/cas 17592186505519 :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-x}{1+x})}" "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1-x}{1+x}\\right)}"] [:db/cas 17592186505814 :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on $x\\ge 0.$ We have $y\\in [0,1)$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,1)$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1}{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1} \\\\ x({e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1) & ={e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1 \\\\ (x-1){e}^{2{y}^{2}} & +(x+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the ${e}^{2{y}^{2}}$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{x+1}{x-1} \\\\ {e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{1+x}{1-x} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we select the positive root. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$" "Let $y=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on $x\\ge 0.$ We have $y\\in [0,1)$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,1)$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1}{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}+