nce $m>0,$ we have $\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{m}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}\\right)}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16-8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16+8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}\\right)}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+|\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $m>0,$ we have $\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{m}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505772 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})}{1-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{\\frac{5+\\sqrt{3}}{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,ln\\,(\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right)}{1-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{\\frac{5+\\sqrt{3}}{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,ln\\,\\left(\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505780 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{(\\frac{2}{1})}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right)}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505782 :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ x & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ x & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505784 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}+50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}-50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+|\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p\\ge 25,$ we have $\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{p}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\left(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}\\right)}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}+50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}-50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}\\right)}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+|\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p\\ge 25,$ we have $\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\left(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{p}{5}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505785 :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we plug $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(\\frac{1}{2})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}){(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(\\frac{1}{2}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{1-x} & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(\\frac{1}{2}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+8{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we plug $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(x-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{1-x} & ⟹ &  & f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+8{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505806 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+4\\sqrt{x}-1}{1-4\\sqrt{x}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{2-4\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+4\\sqrt{x}-1}{1-4\\sqrt{x}+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{2-4\\sqrt{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505807 :question/worked-solution "The exact amount of paint is ${(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}$ liters.\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{20}{9}\\times \\frac{20}{9}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\times 20}{9\\times 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{400}{81}.\n$$" "The exact amount of paint is ${\\left(\\frac{20}{9}\\right)}^{2}$ liters.\n\nWriting ${\\left(\\frac{20}{9}\\right)}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{20}{9}\\right)}^{2} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{20}{9}\\times \\frac{20}{9}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\times 20}{9\\times 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{20}{9}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{400}{81}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505809 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ | $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ | $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505812 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+\\frac{2w+1}{3}}{1-\\frac{2w+1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3+2w+1}{3}}{\\frac{3-2w-1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4+2w}{2-2w}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+w}{1-w}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{2w+1}{3}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+\\frac{2w+1}{3}}{1-\\frac{2w+1}{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\frac{3+2w+1}{3}}{\\frac{3-2w-1}{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4+2w}{2-2w}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2+w}{1-w}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505826 :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+2}=(x+2)^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-(x+2)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(x+2)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=2(1+2)^{-3}=\\frac{2}{27}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}=\\frac{(\\frac{2}{27})}{2}=\\frac{1}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+2}=(x+2)^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-(x+2)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(x+2)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=2(1+2)^{-3}=\\frac{2}{27}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{27}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{1}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505828 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{18}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{(\\frac{2}{1})}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{18}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\left(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right)}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505845 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+|\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we have $\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2{t}^{2}}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}\\right)}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}\\right)}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+|\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we have $\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2{t}^{2}}{2t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506509 :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{36(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((36x+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(1{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+1+2=39.\n$$" "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{36(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((36x+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(1{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+1+2=39.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506513 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+6)^{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({cos}^{4}⁡x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3(x-1)-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(x+6)(x-1)-7(x+6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{-4x-45-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}(4x+45+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(4{x}^{2}+20x-24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+20+(-24)=0.\n$$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+6)^{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({cos}^{4}⁡x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\left(\\frac{3(x-1)-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(x+6)(x-1)-7(x+6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\left(\\frac{-4x-45-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}(4x+45+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(4{x}^{2}+20x-24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+20+(-24)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506518 :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}]}^{1/4} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot (2x)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot (4x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{x}{2({x}^{2}+4)}-\\frac{8x}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{x(2{x}^{2}+1)-16x({x}^{2}+4)}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{-14{x}^{3}-63x}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x(14{x}^{2}+63)}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\sqrt[4]{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}]}^{1/4} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot (2x)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot (4x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{x}{2({x}^{2}+4)}-\\frac{8x}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\left(\\frac{x(2{x}^{2}+1)-16x({x}^{2}+4)}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\left(\\frac{-14{x}^{3}-63x}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x(14{x}^{2}+63)}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\sqrt[4]{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506525 :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-12 & =9z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-9z-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-3z-4) & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-3z-4 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} & =4 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ [{z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}]-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\frac{3}{2},q=\\frac{25}{4}$ and $p+q=\\frac{19}{4}.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |z-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}=4 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=4$ and $z=-1.$" "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-12 & =9z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-9z-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-3z-4) & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-3z-4 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} & =4 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ [{z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}]-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\frac{3}{2},q=\\frac{25}{4}$ and $p+q=\\frac{19}{4}.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |z-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}=4 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=4$ and $z=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186506527 :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot (4{x}^{3}+4x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}+4x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8>0\\text{ }∀x\\in R$, so $|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}[\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot ({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(8{x}^{3}+8x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8))\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot (4{x}^{3}+4x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}+4x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8>0\\text{ }∀x\\in R$, so $|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}[\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot ({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(8{x}^{3}+8x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8))\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506532 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient, product, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3-x)^{3})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}-3\\cdot \\frac{-1}{3-x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4(3-x)+3(x-2)}{(x-2)(3-x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18}{(x-2)(3-x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18)}{(x-2)^{5}(3-x)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient, product, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3-x)^{3})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}-3\\cdot \\frac{-1}{3-x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}\\left(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4(3-x)+3(x-2)}{(x-2)(3-x)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}\\left(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18}{(x-2)(3-x)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18)}{(x-2)^{5}(3-x)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506534 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{(\\frac{A}{4\\pi })}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =18\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & ={(18\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2} \\\\ A & =324\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =324\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =81 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{\\left(\\frac{A}{4\\pi }\\right)}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}\\left(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =18\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & ={(18\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2} \\\\ A & =324\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =324\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =81 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506535 :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10$ and $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =10\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{9}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the puddle is increasing at a rate of $45\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10$ and $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =10\\cdot 2\\pi \\left(\\frac{9}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the puddle is increasing at a rate of $45\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186506542 :question/worked-solution "The variable $a$ is being *divided* by $4.$ To isolate $a$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-4.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{a}{4}\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-2 \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4})\\,\\, & <\\,\\,(-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4})\\,\\, & <\\,\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4}) & <8 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}4)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{a}{4}) & <8 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{a}{4} & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{a}{4} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{4a}{4} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}a}{\\cancel{4}} & <8 \\\\ a & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a<8.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/eb/ebda9e955c2613aa9e65b950ac80ad7ec4e52d6db6fdcce76cc2f89bbc20b0d4.png)" "The variable $a$ is being *divided* by $4.$ To isolate $a$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-4.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{a}{4}\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-2 \\\\ (-4)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{4}\\right)\\,\\, & <\\,\\,(-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-4)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{4}\\right)\\,\\, & <\\,\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{4}\\right) & <8 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}4)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{a}{4}) & <8 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{a}{4} & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{a}{4} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{4a}{4} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}a}{\\cancel{4}} & <8 \\\\ a & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a<8.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/eb/ebda9e955c2613aa9e65b950ac80ad7ec4e52d6db6fdcce76cc2f89bbc20b0d4.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186506548 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-6x+10) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =10 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =10 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =10+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}=-\\frac{10}{4}=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1.$" "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-6x+10) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =10 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}] & =10 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =10+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}=-\\frac{10}{4}=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186506552 :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(x-3)^{8}} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{8/2} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-3)^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{3x(x-3)+4({x}^{2}+1)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{7{x}^{2}-9x+4}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}(\\frac{7{x}^{2}-9x+4}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{1/2}(x-3)^{3}(7{x}^{2}-9x+4) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}(x-3)^{3}(7{x}^{2}-9x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=7+(-9)+4=2.\n$$" "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(x-3)^{8}} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{8/2} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-3)^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}\\left(\\frac{3x(x-3)+4({x}^{2}+1)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}\\left(\\frac{7{x}^{2}-9x+4}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}\\right) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}\\left(\\frac{7{x}^{2}-9x+4}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}\\right) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{1/2}(x-3)^{3}(7{x}^{2}-9x+4) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}(x-3)^{3}(7{x}^{2}-9x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=7+(-9)+4=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506564 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+2)^{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+2x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{3(3{x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{3}+2x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{4({x}^{3}+2x-1)-(x+2)(9{x}^{2}+6)}{(x+2)({x}^{3}+2x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{-5{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+2x-16}{(x+2)({x}^{3}+2x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{3}(-5{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+2x-16)}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+2)^{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+2x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{3(3{x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{3}+2x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}\\left(\\frac{4({x}^{3}+2x-1)-(x+2)(9{x}^{2}+6)}{(x+2)({x}^{3}+2x-1)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}\\left(\\frac{-5{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+2x-16}{(x+2)({x}^{3}+2x-1)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{3}(-5{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+2x-16)}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506577 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+8)^{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{8}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+8}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6(x+8)+5(x+3)-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x(x+3)(x+8)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6x+48+5x+15-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=11+11+24=46.\n$$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+8)^{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{8}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+8}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{6(x+8)+5(x+3)-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x(x+3)(x+8)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{6x+48+5x+15-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=11+11+24=46.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506582 :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2}}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2}}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x+2)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2x}\\cdot (4{x}^{3}+2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{(2{x}^{3}+1)({x}^{2}+2x+2)-(x+1)({x}^{4}+2x)}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{2{x}^{5}+4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+2x+2-({x}^{5}+{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+2x)}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{3/2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+4+2=7.\n$$" "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2}}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2}}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x+2)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2x}\\cdot (4{x}^{3}+2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}\\left(\\frac{(2{x}^{3}+1)({x}^{2}+2x+2)-(x+1)({x}^{4}+2x)}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5}+4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+2x+2-({x}^{5}+{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+2x)}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{3/2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+4+2=7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506585 :question/worked-solution "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x,$ we first replace $y$ with $\\frac{1}{3}x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the last equation is a false statement, we conclude that the hyperbola does not intersect the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x.$" "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x,$ we first replace $y$ with $\\frac{1}{3}x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the last equation is a false statement, we conclude that the hyperbola does not intersect the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186506590 :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{5}{2\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the pool is increasing at a rate of $15\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot 2\\pi \\left(\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the pool is increasing at a rate of $15\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186506591 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+1)^{{x}^{2}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-4x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-(2-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2+4x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+1}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(\\frac{2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4(x+1)}{x+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4(x+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4x+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+(x+2)^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).\n$$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+1)^{{x}^{2}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-4x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-(2-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2+4x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+1}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}\\left(\\frac{2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4(x+1)}{x+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4(x+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4x+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+(x+2)^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506773 :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" "\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186506943 :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" "\\frac{9x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186507105 :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" "\\frac{9x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186507154 :answer/value "-\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" "-\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186507508 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186507508 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A &=-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=\\frac{9}{16\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$" "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A &=-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=\\frac{9}{16\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506822 :answer/value "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ and $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})$" "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$ and $\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186506834 :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$" "$x=0$ and $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186507514 :question/worked-solution "The definitions of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}),\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definitions into the equation  $5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}))-\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x} & =5 \\\\ 2{e}^{x}+3{e}^{-x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (2{e}^{x}+3{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 5 \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}+3 & =5{e}^{x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{x}+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in ${e}^{x}$ that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{x}-3)({e}^{x}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n2{e}^{x}-3=0,\\,{e}^{x}-1=0\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}-3 & =0 &  & \\, & {e}^{x}-1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\frac{3}{2} &  & \\, & {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) &  & \\, & x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$ are the real solutions to the equation." "The definitions of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}),\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definitions into the equation  $5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}))-\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x} & =5 \\\\ 2{e}^{x}+3{e}^{-x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (2{e}^{x}+3{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 5 \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}+3 & =5{e}^{x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{x}+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in ${e}^{x}$ that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{x}-3)({e}^{x}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n2{e}^{x}-3=0,\\,{e}^{x}-1=0\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}-3 & =0 &  & \\, & {e}^{x}-1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\frac{3}{2} &  & \\, & {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) &  & \\, & x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ are the real solutions to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186507404 :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186507408 :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(1)=2$ and $g(1)=1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,3)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (0,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(1)=2$ and $g(1)=1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,3)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (0,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186507419 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y-8}{3} & \\le \\frac{8-2y}{4} \\\\ 12\\cdot (\\frac{2y-8}{3}) & \\le 12\\cdot (\\frac{8-2y}{4}) \\\\ 4\\cdot (2y-8) & \\le 3\\cdot (8-2y) \\\\ 8y-32 & \\le 24-6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y-32 & \\le 24-6y \\\\ 8y-32+6y & \\le 24-6y+6y \\\\ 14y-32 & \\le 24 \\\\ 14y-32+32 & \\le 24+32 \\\\ 14y & \\le 56 \\\\ \\frac{14y}{14} & \\le \\frac{56}{14} \\\\ y & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\le 4.$" "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y-8}{3} & \\le \\frac{8-2y}{4} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2y-8}{3}\\right) & \\le 12\\cdot \\left(\\frac{8-2y}{4}\\right) \\\\ 4\\cdot (2y-8) & \\le 3\\cdot (8-2y) \\\\ 8y-32 & \\le 24-6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y-32 & \\le 24-6y \\\\ 8y-32+6y & \\le 24-6y+6y \\\\ 14y-32 & \\le 24 \\\\ 14y-32+32 & \\le 24+32 \\\\ 14y & \\le 56 \\\\ \\frac{14y}{14} & \\le \\frac{56}{14} \\\\ y & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\le 4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507426 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-7x}{5} & \\ge 2 \\\\ 5\\cdot (\\frac{1-7x}{5}) & \\ge 5\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot (\\frac{1-7x}{\\cancel{5}}) & \\ge 10 \\\\ 1-7x & \\ge 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-7x & \\ge 10 \\\\ 1-7x-1 & \\ge 10-1 \\\\ -7x & \\ge 9 \\\\ \\frac{-7x}{-7} & \\,\\,\\le \\,\\,\\frac{9}{-7} \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le -\\frac{9}{7}.$" "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-7x}{5} & \\ge 2 \\\\ 5\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-7x}{5}\\right) & \\ge 5\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-7x}{\\cancel{5}}\\right) & \\ge 10 \\\\ 1-7x & \\ge 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-7x & \\ge 10 \\\\ 1-7x-1 & \\ge 10-1 \\\\ -7x & \\ge 9 \\\\ \\frac{-7x}{-7} & \\,\\,\\le \\,\\,\\frac{9}{-7} \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le -\\frac{9}{7}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507442 :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186507444 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent a sandwich price. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{1.10}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu )=0.90.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.45 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.5+0.45 \\\\ P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)=1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & =1.6449 \\\\ 0.20\\mu  & =1.809\\,39 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.809\\,39}{0.20} \\\\  & ≈9.05\\,\\text{dollars}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $X$ represent a sandwich price. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{1.10}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu )=0.90.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.45 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.5+0.45 \\\\ P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}\\right) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)=1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & =1.6449 \\\\ 0.20\\mu  & =1.809\\,39 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.809\\,39}{0.20} \\\\  & ≈9.05\\,\\text{dollars}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186507452 :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{f(x)-f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{f(x)-f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,{f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507456 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t-6}{2}\\le 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{5t-6}{2}) & \\le 2\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5t-6}{\\cancel{2}}) & \\le 2 \\\\ 5t-6 & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5t-6 & \\le 2 \\\\ 5t-6+6 & \\le 2+6 \\\\ 5t & \\le 8 \\\\ \\frac{5t}{5} & \\le \\frac{8}{5} \\\\ t & \\le \\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t\\le \\frac{8}{5}.$" "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t-6}{2}\\le 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5t-6}{2}\\right) & \\le 2\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5t-6}{\\cancel{2}}\\right) & \\le 2 \\\\ 5t-6 & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5t-6 & \\le 2 \\\\ 5t-6+6 & \\le 2+6 \\\\ 5t & \\le 8 \\\\ \\frac{5t}{5} & \\le \\frac{8}{5} \\\\ t & \\le \\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t\\le \\frac{8}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507457 :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+\\frac{25}{36}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{5}{6}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+\\frac{25}{36}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}x+{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{5}{6}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186507462 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0) &=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0) &=4L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1 &=-{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3 &=4{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=-\\frac{1}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)-3=4(-\\frac{1}{s+1})+2{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s-1}{(s+1)(s-2)}.\n$$" "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0) &=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0) &=4L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1 &=-{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3 &=4{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=-\\frac{1}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)-3=4\\left(-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\right)+2{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s-1}{(s+1)(s-2)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186507467 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4y-9}{15} & <\\frac{y}{3} \\\\ 15\\cdot (\\frac{4y-9}{15}) & <15\\cdot (\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\ 4y-9 & <5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-9 & <5y \\\\ 4y-9-5y & <5y-5y \\\\ -9-y & <0 \\\\ -9-y+9 & <0+9 \\\\ -y & <9 \\\\ \\frac{-y}{-1} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{9}{-1} \\\\ y & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-9.$" "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4y-9}{15} & <\\frac{y}{3} \\\\ 15\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4y-9}{15}\\right) & <15\\cdot \\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right) \\\\ 4y-9 & <5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-9 & <5y \\\\ 4y-9-5y & <5y-5y \\\\ -9-y & <0 \\\\ -9-y+9 & <0+9 \\\\ -y & <9 \\\\ \\frac{-y}{-1} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{9}{-1} \\\\ y & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507471 :question/worked-solution "At $x=-1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(-1)+3b \\\\  & =-2a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ -5 & =-2a+3b \\\\ 2a-3b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(2)+3b \\\\  & =4a+3b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 4a+3b & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2a-3b &=5 \\\\ 4a+3b &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding both equations we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & =9 \\\\ a & =\\frac{9}{6} \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can replace $a$ by $\\frac{3}{2}$ into the first equation. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{3}{2})-3b & =5 \\\\ 3-3b & =5 \\\\ -3b & =2 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $x=2$ for $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $b=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" "At $x=-1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(-1)+3b \\\\  & =-2a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ -5 & =-2a+3b \\\\ 2a-3b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(2)+3b \\\\  & =4a+3b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 4a+3b & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2a-3b &=5 \\\\ 4a+3b &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding both equations we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & =9 \\\\ a & =\\frac{9}{6} \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can replace $a$ by $\\frac{3}{2}$ into the first equation. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-3b & =5 \\\\ 3-3b & =5 \\\\ -3b & =2 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $x=2$ for $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $b=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507475 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-3p}{4} & <2p+1 \\\\ 4\\cdot (\\frac{2-3p}{4}) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot (\\frac{2-3p}{\\cancel{4}}) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ 2-3p & <8p+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3p & <8p+4 \\\\ 2-3p-8p & <8p+4-8p \\\\ 2-11p & <4 \\\\ 2-11p-2 & <4-2 \\\\ -11p & <2 \\\\ \\frac{-11p}{-11} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{2}{-11} \\\\ p & >-\\frac{2}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p>-\\frac{2}{11}.$" "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-3p}{4} & <2p+1 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2-3p}{4}\\right) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2-3p}{\\cancel{4}}\\right) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ 2-3p & <8p+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3p & <8p+4 \\\\ 2-3p-8p & <8p+4-8p \\\\ 2-11p & <4 \\\\ 2-11p-2 & <4-2 \\\\ -11p & <2 \\\\ \\frac{-11p}{-11} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{2}{-11} \\\\ p & >-\\frac{2}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p>-\\frac{2}{11}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507477 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-4}{8} & \\le \\frac{2a}{6} \\\\ 24\\cdot (\\frac{2a-4}{8}) & \\le 24\\cdot (\\frac{2a}{6}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (2a-4) & \\le 4\\cdot 2a \\\\ 6a-12 & \\le 8a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a-12 & \\le 8a \\\\ 6a-12-8a & \\le 8a-8a \\\\ -12-2a & \\le 0 \\\\ -12-2a+12 & \\le 0+12 \\\\ -2a & \\le 12 \\\\ \\frac{-2a}{-2} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{12}{-2} \\\\ a & \\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a\\ge -6.$" "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-4}{8} & \\le \\frac{2a}{6} \\\\ 24\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2a-4}{8}\\right) & \\le 24\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2a}{6}\\right) \\\\ 3\\cdot (2a-4) & \\le 4\\cdot 2a \\\\ 6a-12 & \\le 8a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a-12 & \\le 8a \\\\ 6a-12-8a & \\le 8a-8a \\\\ -12-2a & \\le 0 \\\\ -12-2a+12 & \\le 0+12 \\\\ -2a & \\le 12 \\\\ \\frac{-2a}{-2} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{12}{-2} \\\\ a & \\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a\\ge -6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507489 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected tree. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>50).$  \n\nWe know that $P(X<45)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<45) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 45-\\mu  & =-5.396 \\\\ \\mu  & =50.396.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(50<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>50) & =P(Z>\\frac{50-50.396}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.05) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.05) \\\\  & =1-0.4801 \\\\  & ≈51.99%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $51.99%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet." "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected tree. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>50).$  \n\nWe know that $P(X<45)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<45) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}\\right) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 45-\\mu  & =-5.396 \\\\ \\mu  & =50.396.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(50<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>50) & =P(Z>\\frac{50-50.396}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.05) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.05) \\\\  & =1-0.4801 \\\\  & ≈51.99%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $51.99%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet."] [:db/cas 17592186507491 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z+9}{3} & \\ge -z-1 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2z+9}{3}) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2z+9}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ 2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3 \\\\ 2z+9+3z & \\ge -3z-3+3z \\\\ 5z+9 & \\ge -3 \\\\ 5z+9-9 & \\ge -3-9 \\\\ 5z & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\ge \\frac{-12}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge -\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge -\\frac{12}{5}.$" "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z+9}{3} & \\ge -z-1 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2z+9}{3}\\right) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2z+9}{\\cancel{3}}\\right) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ 2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3 \\\\ 2z+9+3z & \\ge -3z-3+3z \\\\ 5z+9 & \\ge -3 \\\\ 5z+9-9 & \\ge -3-9 \\\\ 5z & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\ge \\frac{-12}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge -\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge -\\frac{12}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507499 :question/worked-solution "The variable $z$ is being *divided* by $6.$ To isolate $z$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-6$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-\\frac{z}{6} \\\\ (-6)\\cdot 4\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\,\\, & >(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24 & >(\\cancel{-}6)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{6z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{\\cancel{6}z}{\\cancel{6}} \\\\ -24 & >z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nz<-24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z<-24.$" "The variable $z$ is being *divided* by $6.$ To isolate $z$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-6$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-\\frac{z}{6} \\\\ (-6)\\cdot 4\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{z}{6}\\right) \\\\ -24\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{z}{6}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\,\\, & >(-6)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{z}{6}\\right) \\\\ -24 & >(\\cancel{-}6)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{6z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{\\cancel{6}z}{\\cancel{6}} \\\\ -24 & >z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nz<-24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z<-24.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507503 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5z+2}{3} & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{5z+2}{3}) & \\ge 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{5z+2}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5z+2 & \\ge 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5z+2-2 & \\ge 12-2 \\\\ 5z & \\ge 10 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\ge \\frac{10}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge 2.$" "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5z+2}{3} & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5z+2}{3}\\right) & \\ge 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5z+2}{\\cancel{3}}\\right) & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5z+2 & \\ge 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5z+2-2 & \\ge 12-2 \\\\ 5z & \\ge 10 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\ge \\frac{10}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge 2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507506 :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+2}{3} & <\\frac{y+3}{2} \\\\ 6\\cdot (\\frac{y+2}{3}) & <6\\cdot (\\frac{y+3}{2}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (y+2) & <3\\cdot (y+3) \\\\ 2y+4 & <3y+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+4 & <3y+9 \\\\ 2y+4-3y & <3y+9-3y \\\\ 4-y & <9 \\\\ 4-y-4 & <9-4 \\\\ -y & <5 \\\\ \\frac{-y}{-1} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{5}{-1} \\\\ y & >-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-5.$" "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+2}{3} & <\\frac{y+3}{2} \\\\ 6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{y+2}{3}\\right) & <6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{y+3}{2}\\right) \\\\ 2\\cdot (y+2) & <3\\cdot (y+3) \\\\ 2y+4 & <3y+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+4 & <3y+9 \\\\ 2y+4-3y & <3y+9-3y \\\\ 4-y & <9 \\\\ 4-y-4 & <9-4 \\\\ -y & <5 \\\\ \\frac{-y}{-1} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{5}{-1} \\\\ y & >-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507512 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definition into the equation $8\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}) & =1 \\\\ 8({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\ 8{e}^{x}-8{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\ 7{e}^{x} & =9{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x}$ and solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot 7{e}^{x} & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 9{e}^{-x} \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =\\frac{9}{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7}) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only real solution is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7}).\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definition into the equation $8\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\left(\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\right) & =1 \\\\ 8({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\ 8{e}^{x}-8{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\ 7{e}^{x} & =9{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x}$ and solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot 7{e}^{x} & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 9{e}^{-x} \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =\\frac{9}{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only real solution is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186507519 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{3{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0) &=3L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0) &=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4 &=3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+2 &=-2{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)-4=3{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{4}{s-3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)+2=-2(\\frac{4}{s-3})+{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-2s-2}{(s-1)(s-3)}.\n$$" "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{3{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0) &=3L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0) &=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4 &=3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+2 &=-2{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)-4=3{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{4}{s-3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)+2=-2\\left(\\frac{4}{s-3}\\right)+{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-2s-2}{(s-1)(s-3)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508043 :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{3}{x})|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(3-\\frac{3}{a}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=3$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1 \\\\  & =3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =3+3-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{3}{x}\\right)|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(3-\\frac{3}{a}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=3$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1 \\\\  & =3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =3+3-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508063 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$" "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186508063 :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/a5/a5b088c08da85c389cb96add374186a898793ec23cf0a3e39270c292fe3d606d.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{4}.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{1}{4}),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/d5/d563be7169471498c6e0193e3be8493ae6ba119fc3fc7b74b397454b65e6cf57.png)" "First we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/a5/a5b088c08da85c389cb96add374186a898793ec23cf0a3e39270c292fe3d606d.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{4}.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{1}{4}),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/d5/d563be7169471498c6e0193e3be8493ae6ba119fc3fc7b74b397454b65e6cf57.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186508072 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}?$" "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4{\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)}^{x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186508072 :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/d3/d379a1509beb5b611374d49449e23c386c78d20764ddadfd1b28fc2a6964a818.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,4),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/48/48b94fdedbc1f66e8b018b5fafb7e2ee04d5c1b9c558c1e1de42f4d6f3428c64.png)" "First we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/d3/d379a1509beb5b611374d49449e23c386c78d20764ddadfd1b28fc2a6964a818.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,4),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/48/48b94fdedbc1f66e8b018b5fafb7e2ee04d5c1b9c558c1e1de42f4d6f3428c64.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186508080 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to +\\,\\infty $ as $x\\to +\\,\\infty $" "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to +\\,\\infty $ as $x\\to +\\,\\infty $"] [:db/cas 17592186508080 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/45/4531a531fb98f092751cec4552d6ead2bf828759842214f030618dba1ea85893.png)\n\n \n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false, since the domain of the function $f(x)={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x>0,$ the graph increases without a bound as $x$ increases to infinity.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/45/4531a531fb98f092751cec4552d6ead2bf828759842214f030618dba1ea85893.png)\n\n \n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false, since the domain of the function $f(x)={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x>0,$ the graph increases without a bound as $x$ increases to infinity.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."] [:db/cas 17592186508084 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its range is $y>\\frac{4}{5}$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its range is $y>\\frac{4}{5}$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $"] [:db/cas 17592186508084 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/82/8250b7e30d7960f7c06d0e254e8bad97546e959be1ad3128eb491800b6e91533.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers, so the range is  $y>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/82/8250b7e30d7960f7c06d0e254e8bad97546e959be1ad3128eb491800b6e91533.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers, so the range is  $y>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."] [:db/cas 17592186508088 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(-1,4).$\n\nIII. It's an increasing function" "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(-1,4).$\n\nIII. It's an increasing function"] [:db/cas 17592186508088 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/5d/5d97ca13c1289558c6bb8e8ce50fb1ef5745f54acf27346a32d6451b1bb7f9bb.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x,$ so the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(-1)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-1}=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/5d/5d97ca13c1289558c6bb8e8ce50fb1ef5745f54acf27346a32d6451b1bb7f9bb.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f(x)={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x,$ so the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(-1)={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{-1}=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."] [:db/cas 17592186508090 :question/problem "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{x}?$" "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}^{x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186508090 :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept of $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x>0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0.$ \n\nWhen $x=1,$ we have $y={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,\\frac{5}{4}).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/92/929a671186aad590fe4f771d9facb822b40fe43b77579720edd38fb8f3d22f25.png)" "The graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept of $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x>0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0.$ \n\nWhen $x=1,$ we have $y={\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,\\frac{5}{4}).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/92/929a671186aad590fe4f771d9facb822b40fe43b77579720edd38fb8f3d22f25.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186508104 :question/problem "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$" "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186508104 :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{-1}=5.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(-1,5).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png)" "The graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{-1}=5.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(-1,5).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186508187 :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =-5 \\\\ y & =-5-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=-5-3x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+3y & =2 \\\\ 3x+3(-5-3x) & =2 \\\\ 3x-15-9x & =2 \\\\ -6x-15 & =2 \\\\ -6x & =17 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=-\\frac{17}{6}$ into $y=-5-3x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5-3(-\\frac{17}{6}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-\\frac{17}{6},\\,y=\\frac{7}{2}.$" "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =-5 \\\\ y & =-5-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=-5-3x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+3y & =2 \\\\ 3x+3(-5-3x) & =2 \\\\ 3x-15-9x & =2 \\\\ -6x-15 & =2 \\\\ -6x & =17 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=-\\frac{17}{6}$ into $y=-5-3x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5-3\\left(-\\frac{17}{6}\\right) \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-\\frac{17}{6},\\,y=\\frac{7}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508204 :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ y & =6+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=6+2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y & =8 \\\\ -3x+4(6+2x) & =8 \\\\ -3x+24+8x & =8 \\\\ 5x+24 & =8 \\\\ 5x & =-16 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=-\\frac{16}{5}$ into $y=6+2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6+2(-\\frac{16}{5}) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-\\frac{16}{5},\\,y=-\\frac{2}{5}.$" "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ y & =6+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=6+2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y & =8 \\\\ -3x+4(6+2x) & =8 \\\\ -3x+24+8x & =8 \\\\ 5x+24 & =8 \\\\ 5x & =-16 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=-\\frac{16}{5}$ into $y=6+2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6+2\\left(-\\frac{16}{5}\\right) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-\\frac{16}{5},\\,y=-\\frac{2}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508227 :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-1-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=-1-2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-2y & =5 \\\\ 5x-2(-1-2x) & =5 \\\\ 5x+2+4x & =5 \\\\ 9x+2 & =5 \\\\ 9x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into $y=-1-2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1-2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=-\\frac{5}{3}.$" "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-1-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=-1-2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-2y & =5 \\\\ 5x-2(-1-2x) & =5 \\\\ 5x+2+4x & =5 \\\\ 9x+2 & =5 \\\\ 9x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into $y=-1-2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1-2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=-\\frac{5}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508233 :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =5 \\\\ y & =5+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=5+2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =12 \\\\ 2x+2(5+2x) & =12 \\\\ 2x+10+4x & =12 \\\\ 6x+10 & =12 \\\\ 6x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into $y=5+2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=\\frac{17}{3}.$" "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =5 \\\\ y & =5+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=5+2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =12 \\\\ 2x+2(5+2x) & =12 \\\\ 2x+10+4x & =12 \\\\ 6x+10 & =12 \\\\ 6x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into $y=5+2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ y & =\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=\\frac{17}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508605 :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186508660 :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186508963 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508963 :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=6$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 6 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=6$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 6 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508911 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2}.$" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\right)}^{2x+2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508911 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(x+1)+1}{x+1})}^{2x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x+1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\right)}^{2x+2}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\right)}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{(x+1)+1}{x+1}\\right)}^{2x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x+1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\right)}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508912 :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius of the semicircles (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the length of the other side of the rectangle. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2rx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $120\\text{ m},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n120=\\pi r+\\pi r+2x=2\\pi r+2x.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & =2\\pi r+2x \\\\ 2x & =120-2\\pi r \\\\ x & =60-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2r(60-\\pi r) \\\\  & =120r-2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =120-4\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 120-4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =120 \\\\ r & =\\frac{30}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-4\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })=-4\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =60-\\pi (\\frac{30}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =60-30 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=30$ and $2r=\\frac{60}{\\pi },$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+2r=30+\\frac{60}{\\pi }=\\frac{30\\pi +60}{\\pi }.\n$$" "First, let $r$ be the radius of the semicircles (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the length of the other side of the rectangle. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2rx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $120\\text{ m},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n120=\\pi r+\\pi r+2x=2\\pi r+2x.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & =2\\pi r+2x \\\\ 2x & =120-2\\pi r \\\\ x & =60-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2r(60-\\pi r) \\\\  & =120r-2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =120-4\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 120-4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =120 \\\\ r & =\\frac{30}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-4\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}\\left(\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\right)=-4\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}\\left(\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\right)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =60-\\pi \\left(\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\right) \\\\  & =60-30 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=30$ and $2r=\\frac{60}{\\pi },$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+2r=30+\\frac{60}{\\pi }=\\frac{30\\pi +60}{\\pi }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508915 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =4\\cdot 2xz+1\\cdot 2xy+1\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =8xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $36\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 36 & =xz(2z) \\\\ 36 & =2x{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =8xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =8\\,(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}})\\,z+2\\,(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}})\\,(2z)+2(2z)z \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{z}+\\frac{72}{z}+4{z}^{2} \\\\  & =4{z}^{2}+\\frac{216}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 8{z}^{3} & =216 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=8+\\frac{432}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=8+16=24>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units." "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =4\\cdot 2xz+1\\cdot 2xy+1\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =8xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $36\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 36 & =xz(2z) \\\\ 36 & =2x{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =8xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =8\\,\\left(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}}\\right)\\,z+2\\,\\left(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}}\\right)\\,(2z)+2(2z)z \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{z}+\\frac{72}{z}+4{z}^{2} \\\\  & =4{z}^{2}+\\frac{216}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 8{z}^{3} & =216 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=8+\\frac{432}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=8+16=24>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units."] [:db/cas 17592186508919 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=8-2y,\\,z=6-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $8-2y>0$ and $6-2y>0$, hence $y<3.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(8-2y)(y)(6-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-28{y}^{2}+48y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-56y+48,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-56y+48 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-14y+12 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $\\frac{7+\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ since we already know that $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-56.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}≈1.13$ gives the maximum possible volume." "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=8-2y,\\,z=6-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $8-2y>0$ and $6-2y>0$, hence $y<3.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(8-2y)(y)(6-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-28{y}^{2}+48y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-56y+48,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-56y+48 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-14y+12 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $\\frac{7+\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ since we already know that $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-56.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}\\left(\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}\\right)$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}≈1.13$ gives the maximum possible volume."] [:db/cas 17592186508935 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 180 & =2{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}^{2}y & =180 \\\\ y & =\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\  & =10(2z)z+4(2z)(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}})+4\\,(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}})\\,z \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{720}{z}+\\frac{360}{z} \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{1080}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 40{z}^{3} & =1080 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=40+\\frac{2160}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=40+80=120>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units." "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 180 & =2{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}^{2}y & =180 \\\\ y & =\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\  & =10(2z)z+4(2z)\\left(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}}\\right)+4\\,\\left(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}}\\right)\\,z \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{720}{z}+\\frac{360}{z} \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{1080}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 40{z}^{3} & =1080 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=40+\\frac{2160}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=40+80=120>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units."] [:db/cas 17592186508936 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =3-3{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =3-3{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3-3{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =3x+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =3x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =3-3{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =3-3{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3-3{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =3x+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =3x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508937 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(4y)+2xy+2y(4y) \\\\  & =8xy+2xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}})y+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2000}{y}+8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2000}{5} \\\\  & =600\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(4y)+2xy+2y(4y) \\\\  & =8xy+2xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10\\left(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\right)y+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2000}{y}+8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2000}{5} \\\\  & =600\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508949 :question/worked-solution "If the number of alive bacteria reduces by $\\frac{1}{4}$ every minute, then $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the bacteria remains every minute. So the decay factor is $\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=3\\,000$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n\n- $b=\\frac{3}{4}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in minutes.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=3\\,000\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $\\frac{3}{4}.$" "If the number of alive bacteria reduces by $\\frac{1}{4}$ every minute, then $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the bacteria remains every minute. So the decay factor is $\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=3\\,000$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n\n- $b=\\frac{3}{4}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in minutes.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=3\\,000\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $\\frac{3}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508952 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $288$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 288 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{3},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n288=xy(\\frac{x}{3})=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3} & =288 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =864 \\\\ y & =\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(\\frac{x}{3})+2x(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}})(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1728}{x}+\\frac{576}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{2304}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x & =\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3} & =2304 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1728 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=12$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4608}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(12)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}=4>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(12)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=12$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(12) & =\\frac{2}{3}(12)^{2}+\\frac{2304}{12} \\\\  & =96+192 \\\\  & =288\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $288$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 288 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{3},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n288=xy\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3} & =288 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =864 \\\\ y & =\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+2x\\left(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1728}{x}+\\frac{576}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{2304}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x & =\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3} & =2304 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1728 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=12$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4608}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(12)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}=4>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(12)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=12$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(12) & =\\frac{2}{3}(12)^{2}+\\frac{2304}{12} \\\\  & =96+192 \\\\  & =288\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508954 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $[-5,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,5].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{x(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{x+1})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,5],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[5,10].$ In this interval $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[5,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $[-5,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,5].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\left(\\frac{x(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\left(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,5],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[5,10].$ In this interval $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[5,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186508958 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is exactly $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4z{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $z$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ z & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2(4y)(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}})+2(4y)y+2y(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,600}{y}+8{y}^{2}+\\frac{400}{y} \\\\  & =8{y}^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3}-2\\,000 & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2\\,000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4\\,000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{5}=600.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is exactly $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4z{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $z$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ z & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2(4y)\\left(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\right)+2(4y)y+2y\\left(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,600}{y}+8{y}^{2}+\\frac{400}{y} \\\\  & =8{y}^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3}-2\\,000 & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2\\,000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4\\,000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{5}=600.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508959 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x}.$" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3x+1}{3x}\\right)}^{6x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508959 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $3x=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3x+1}{3x}\\right)}^{6x}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3x+1}{3x}\\right)}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $3x=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3x+1}{3x}\\right)}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508964 :question/worked-solution "Since the value of the bicycle reduces by $\\frac{1}{5}$ per year, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of its value remains every year. \n\nLet's calculate the value of the bicycle after every year:\n\n- After $1$ year, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot (\\frac{4}{5})$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{4}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nSo after $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $$256.$" "Since the value of the bicycle reduces by $\\frac{1}{5}$ per year, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of its value remains every year. \n\nLet's calculate the value of the bicycle after every year:\n\n- After $1$ year, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{4}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nSo after $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $$256.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508967 :question/problem "Due to a quarantine initiated in March $2020$, the number of reservations per night in a hotel decreased by one-quarter each month. By the end of $2020,$ the number of reservations per night decreased to $75\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{9}.$ What does the factor $75$ mean in this context?" "Due to a quarantine initiated in March $2020$, the number of reservations per night in a hotel decreased by one-quarter each month. By the end of $2020,$ the number of reservations per night decreased to $75\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{9}.$ What does the factor $75$ mean in this context?"] [:db/cas 17592186508968 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\  & =16{e}^{-4x}(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}) \\\\  & =8{e}^{-4x}({e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =8-8{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8-8{e}^{-8x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 8-8{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =8x+{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =8x{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\  & =16{e}^{-4x}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8{e}^{-4x}({e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =8-8{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8-8{e}^{-8x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 8-8{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =8x+{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =8x{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508976 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}.$" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2}\\right)}^{x-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508976 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(2x-2)+1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $2(x-1)=n$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-1) & =n \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{n}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2}\\right)}^{x-1}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2}\\right)}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{(2x-2)+1}{2x-2}\\right)}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $2(x-1)=n$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-1) & =n \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{n}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2}\\right)}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508981 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=15-2y,\\,z=8-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $15-2y>0$ and $8-2y>0$, hence $y<4.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(15-2y)(y)(8-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-46{y}^{2}+120y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-92y+120,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-92y+120 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-23y+30 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-6) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $6$ since we already know that $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-92.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{5}{3})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume." "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=15-2y,\\,z=8-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $15-2y>0$ and $8-2y>0$, hence $y<4.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(15-2y)(y)(8-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-46{y}^{2}+120y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-92y+120,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-92y+120 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-23y+30 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-6) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $6$ since we already know that $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-92.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume."] [:db/cas 17592186508982 :question/problem "A five gallons water bottle is leaking its content. The number of gallons of water left inside the bottle after $t$ minutes is given by the formula $W(t)=5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{t}.$ How many gallons of water will be inside the bottle after $3$ minutes?" "A five gallons water bottle is leaking its content. The number of gallons of water left inside the bottle after $t$ minutes is given by the formula $W(t)=5\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{t}.$ How many gallons of water will be inside the bottle after $3$ minutes?"] [:db/cas 17592186508982 :question/worked-solution "To get the number of gallons of water inside the bottle after $3$ minutes, we evaluate the function at $t=3.$ Substituting $t=3$ into the function $W(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{125} \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after $3$ minutes, there will be $0.04$ gallons of water inside the bottle." "To get the number of gallons of water inside the bottle after $3$ minutes, we evaluate the function at $t=3.$ Substituting $t=3$ into the function $W(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =5\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{125} \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after $3$ minutes, there will be $0.04$ gallons of water inside the bottle."] [:db/cas 17592186508984 :question/worked-solution "First,  let $r$ the radius of the upper semicircle (top) and $x$ the radius of the lateral semicircle (right), hence the dimensions of the rectangle are $2r$ and $2x.$ We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(2r)(2x)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $200$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n200=\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}200 & =\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r \\\\ x(\\pi +2) & =200-r(\\pi +2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4r(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =4(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+4r(-1)+\\pi r+\\pi (\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{800}{\\pi +2}-4r-4r+\\pi r-\\frac{200\\pi }{\\pi +2}+\\pi r \\\\  & =-8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} & =0 \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =-\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =\\frac{200\\pi -800}{\\pi +2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100\\pi -400}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100(\\pi -4)}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-8+2\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})=-8+2\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-\\frac{100}{\\pi +2} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the side of the field with the largest area is $2x=\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}.$" "First,  let $r$ the radius of the upper semicircle (top) and $x$ the radius of the lateral semicircle (right), hence the dimensions of the rectangle are $2r$ and $2x.$ We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(2r)(2x)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $200$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n200=\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}200 & =\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r \\\\ x(\\pi +2) & =200-r(\\pi +2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4r(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =4(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+4r(-1)+\\pi r+\\pi (\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{800}{\\pi +2}-4r-4r+\\pi r-\\frac{200\\pi }{\\pi +2}+\\pi r \\\\  & =-8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} & =0 \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =-\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =\\frac{200\\pi -800}{\\pi +2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100\\pi -400}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100(\\pi -4)}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-8+2\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}\\left(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\right)=-8+2\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}\\left(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\right)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-\\frac{100}{\\pi +2} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the side of the field with the largest area is $2x=\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508985 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $50\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 50 & =3{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}y & =50 \\\\ y & =\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\ C & =10(3z)z+4(3z)(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}})+4(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}})z \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{200}{z}+\\frac{200}{3z} \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{800}{3z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 180{z}^{3} & =800 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =\\frac{800}{180} \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=60+\\frac{1\\,600}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})$ is positive, we conclude that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$" "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $50\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 50 & =3{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}y & =50 \\\\ y & =\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\ C & =10(3z)z+4(3z)\\left(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}}\\right)z \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{200}{z}+\\frac{200}{3z} \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{800}{3z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 180{z}^{3} & =800 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =\\frac{800}{180} \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=60+\\frac{1\\,600}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})$ is positive, we conclude that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$"] [:db/cas 17592186508988 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $243$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 243 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{2},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n243=xy(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2} & =243 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =486 \\\\ y & =\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(\\frac{x}{2})+2x(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}})(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{972}{x}+\\frac{486}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2{x}^{3} & =1458 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =729 \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=9$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=2+\\frac{2916}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(9)=2+4=6>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(9)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=9$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(9) & ={9}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{9} \\\\  & =243\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $243$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 243 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{2},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n243=xy\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2} & =243 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =486 \\\\ y & =\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+2x\\left(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{972}{x}+\\frac{486}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2{x}^{3} & =1458 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =729 \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=9$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=2+\\frac{2916}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(9)=2+4=6>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(9)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=9$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(9) & ={9}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{9} \\\\  & =243\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508989 :question/worked-solution "Since the number of mice reduced by $\\frac{5}{6}$ per day, then only $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the mice from the previous day remained.\n\nSo we have:\n\n- At the start, there were $720$ mice.\n\n- After $1$ day, there were $720\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6})$ mice.\n\n- After $2$ days, there were $720\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}$ mice.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n720\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $2$ days only $20$ mice remained." "Since the number of mice reduced by $\\frac{5}{6}$ per day, then only $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the mice from the previous day remained.\n\nSo we have:\n\n- At the start, there were $720$ mice.\n\n- After $1$ day, there were $720\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)$ mice.\n\n- After $2$ days, there were $720\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}$ mice.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n720\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $2$ days only $20$ mice remained."] [:db/cas 17592186508995 :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-2} \\\\  & =4{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+6{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.691\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{4-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{4-2} \\\\  & =4{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3}+6{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.691\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186508998 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the side length $x$ of the tray is given by\n\n$$\nx=10-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have that $10-2y>0$ hence $y<5.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =(10-2y)^{2}y \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-40{y}^{2}+100y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-80y+100,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-80y+100 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-20y+25 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $5$ since we already know that $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-80.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{5}{3})=-40$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume." "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the side length $x$ of the tray is given by\n\n$$\nx=10-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have that $10-2y>0$ hence $y<5.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =(10-2y)^{2}y \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-40{y}^{2}+100y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-80y+100,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-80y+100 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-20y+25 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $5$ since we already know that $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-80.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)=-40$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume."] [:db/cas 17592186509624 :question/worked-solution "To compute $-2\\overset{\\to }{A},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\overset{\\to }{A} & =-2\\cdot ⟨-4,- \\frac{7}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-2)\\cdot (-4),(-2)\\cdot (- \\frac{7}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute $-2\\overset{\\to }{A},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\overset{\\to }{A} & =-2\\cdot ⟨-4,- \\frac{7}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-2)\\cdot (-4),(-2)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186509755 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186509771 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186509787 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186510090 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186510372 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{2}+4) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -4x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -4x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-24(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ -4x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{2}+4) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ -4x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ -4x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-24(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ -4x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510371 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{24}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{A}=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}^{Q}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (4\\sqrt{3})=-4.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{24}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{A}=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}^{Q}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)\\cdot (4\\sqrt{3})=-4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186510401 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.3+1.2+(-2.6)+0.4 \\\\  & =-0.7 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.3)^{2}+(1.2)^{2}+(-2.6)^{2}+(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =8.45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(8.45-2(-0.7)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,-4.225\\,-\\,0.7\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma }\\right)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.3+1.2+(-2.6)+0.4 \\\\  & =-0.7 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.3)^{2}+(1.2)^{2}+(-2.6)^{2}+(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =8.45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(8.45-2(-0.7)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,-4.225\\,-\\,0.7\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510406 :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $6\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}(\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}) \\\\ V(r) & =(3r-{r}^{3})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(3-3{r}^{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (3-3{r}^{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 3 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =1 \\\\ r & =1\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=1$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=-6\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(1)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=1$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=1\\,\\text{ft}.$" "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $6\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\left(\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}\\right) \\\\ V(r) & =(3r-{r}^{3})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(3-3{r}^{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (3-3{r}^{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 3 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =1 \\\\ r & =1\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=1$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=-6\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(1)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=1$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=1\\,\\text{ft}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186510424 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/95/959050ee306a96db3745f84bc7095d4e83a23ceadd819f0b749c04b20d5778d2.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 1.5+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3\\pi rh+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $72\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n72\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =3\\pi r(\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}})+0.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{216\\pi }{r}+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r & =0 \\\\ \\pi r & =\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ \\pi {r}^{3} & =216\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =216 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{216} \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=6\\,\\text{ft}.$" "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/95/959050ee306a96db3745f84bc7095d4e83a23ceadd819f0b749c04b20d5778d2.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 1.5+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3\\pi rh+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $72\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n72\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =3\\pi r\\left(\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}}\\right)+0.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{216\\pi }{r}+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r & =0 \\\\ \\pi r & =\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ \\pi {r}^{3} & =216\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =216 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{216} \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=6\\,\\text{ft}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186510427 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/33/33c90b487d2b3036068acf4b771d2ca6ae95e8e8446b706b85fed058dc12a0bf.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $2\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =4\\,000\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =1\\,000 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} \\\\ r & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=10$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=10\\,\\text{cm}.$" "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/33/33c90b487d2b3036068acf4b771d2ca6ae95e8e8446b706b85fed058dc12a0bf.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $2\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r\\left(\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}}\\right)+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =4\\,000\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =1\\,000 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} \\\\ r & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=10$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=10\\,\\text{cm}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186510441 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/7f/7fe5dad64f7c065e693ed407e0f291ec995b4823a0356e11055d20a0ca89a53b.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $8\\pi \\,{in}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n8\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}})+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =16\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ r & =2\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=2$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}},$ we substitute $r=2\\,\\text{in}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(2) & =\\frac{16\\pi }{2}+\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi +4\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/7f/7fe5dad64f7c065e693ed407e0f291ec995b4823a0356e11055d20a0ca89a53b.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $8\\pi \\,{in}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n8\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r\\left(\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}}\\right)+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =16\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ r & =2\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=2$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}},$ we substitute $r=2\\,\\text{in}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(2) & =\\frac{16\\pi }{2}+\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi +4\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510450 :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $64\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=64\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =64\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{64\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{64-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{64-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(64r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=\\frac{(64-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(64-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 64 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{64}{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3})<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}.$" "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $64\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=64\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =64\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{64\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{64-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{64-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(64r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=\\frac{(64-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(64-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 64 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{64}{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}\\left(\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186510451 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/bb/bb9c356aadbb184aaa49c1fa1867da82d64ef40db9048c752c2a91d20704392a.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $128\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n128\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{128}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{128}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{256\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =256\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =64 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{ft}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =\\frac{256\\pi }{4}+2\\pi (4)^{2} \\\\  & =301.592… \\\\  & ≈302\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=302\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$" "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/bb/bb9c356aadbb184aaa49c1fa1867da82d64ef40db9048c752c2a91d20704392a.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $128\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n128\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{128}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r\\left(\\frac{128}{{r}^{2}}\\right)+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{256\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =256\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =64 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{ft}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =\\frac{256\\pi }{4}+2\\pi (4)^{2} \\\\  & =301.592… \\\\  & ≈302\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=302\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186510452 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{A}=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{Q}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=2.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{A}=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{Q}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186510462 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/f3/f32cf2ca384f0f1b676e77e74dd4f02484f2ba585710fee88df6951607081a42.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 6+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 1.50 \\\\  & =12\\pi rh+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $\\frac{27}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{4}\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{27}{4{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =12\\pi r(\\frac{27}{4{r}^{2}})+1.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{r}+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 3\\pi r & =\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 3\\pi {r}^{3} & =81\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=3\\,\\text{in}.$" "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/f3/f32cf2ca384f0f1b676e77e74dd4f02484f2ba585710fee88df6951607081a42.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 6+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 1.50 \\\\  & =12\\pi rh+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $\\frac{27}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{4}\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{27}{4{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =12\\pi r\\left(\\frac{27}{4{r}^{2}}\\right)+1.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{r}+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 3\\pi r & =\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 3\\pi {r}^{3} & =81\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=3\\,\\text{in}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186510468 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/55/55de2c06ea57724513e450cbf49a0477d63cd75bac601d5d95f0ba7e366852ff.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 7.50+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 2.50 \\\\  & =15\\pi rh+2.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $\\frac{125}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{yd}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{125}{3}\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{125}{3{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =15\\pi r(\\frac{125}{3{r}^{2}})+2.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{625\\pi }{r}+2.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+5\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+5\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 5\\pi r & =\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 5\\pi {r}^{3} & =625\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =125 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=5$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=5\\,\\text{yd}.$" "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius,