$\nh=\\frac{100}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =5\\pi r\\left(\\frac{100}{{r}^{2}}\\right)+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{500\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =500\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =125 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\ r & =5\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=5$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=5\\,\\text{m}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186510472 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/ce/cec96bdd2335ff5635d11c4ea6016257b35bd5450778a70ecc1f5e0156ccfe9e.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $686\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n686\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{686}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{686}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =1\\,372\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =343 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{343} \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<7$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>7$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=7$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=7\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=7\\,\\text{mm}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(7) & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{7}+2\\pi (7)^{2} \\\\  & =294\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=294\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.$" "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/ce/cec96bdd2335ff5635d11c4ea6016257b35bd5450778a70ecc1f5e0156ccfe9e.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $686\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n686\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{686}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r\\left(\\frac{686}{{r}^{2}}\\right)+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =1\\,372\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =343 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{343} \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<7$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>7$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=7$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=7\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=7\\,\\text{mm}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(7) & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{7}+2\\pi (7)^{2} \\\\  & =294\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=294\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186510476 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/63/6393c2cbff5d727841ca78ac067ff7067114d6db8754454a30584f38e4a4ea87.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 4.50+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 1.50 \\\\  & =9\\pi rh+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $9\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n9\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =9\\pi r(\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}})+1.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{r}+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 3\\pi r & =\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 3\\pi {r}^{3} & =81\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=3\\,\\text{ft}.$" "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/63/6393c2cbff5d727841ca78ac067ff7067114d6db8754454a30584f38e4a4ea87.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 4.50+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 1.50 \\\\  & =9\\pi rh+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $9\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n9\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =9\\pi r\\left(\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}\\right)+1.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{r}+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 3\\pi r & =\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 3\\pi {r}^{3} & =81\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=3\\,\\text{ft}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186510569 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{9} & -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{7})-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{56}-(-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{113}{280}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{9} & -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{9}{7}\\right)-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{56}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{113}{280}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510573 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{7} & -\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{5})-(-\\frac{9}{5})\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}-(-\\frac{9}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{7} & -\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{9}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}-\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510575 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}\\cdot (-\\frac{8}{3})-(-\\frac{4}{7})\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{27}-(-\\frac{8}{49}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1784}{1323}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{4}{7}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{27}-\\left(-\\frac{8}{49}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1784}{1323}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510576 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{5} & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{4})-\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{28}-(-\\frac{21}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{513}{140}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{5} & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)-\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{28}-\\left(-\\frac{21}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{513}{140}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510727 :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af413350ef3c9edde0eebf49cb8f37c8b20791f4bb01e04adaacaa0c378d28a1.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{43}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43\\cdot 6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/af/af413350ef3c9edde0eebf49cb8f37c8b20791f4bb01e04adaacaa0c378d28a1.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{43}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43\\cdot 6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510730 :question/worked-solution "![](images/51/51778a4029b91dbbd51014a8f4a3e7e1fc772efe96058f41b21bbf408f113cce.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{18}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18\\cdot 7}}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{126}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/51/51778a4029b91dbbd51014a8f4a3e7e1fc772efe96058f41b21bbf408f113cce.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{18}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18\\cdot 7}}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{126}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510733 :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af1047be250d877d836851da681e583ada8f01d2022e5c9671aa83173ed23fa3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{39}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39\\cdot 10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/af/af1047be250d877d836851da681e583ada8f01d2022e5c9671aa83173ed23fa3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{39}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39\\cdot 10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510744 :question/worked-solution "![](images/ba/bad705982f073623644ed540e26cfad849b2dd078c5c64772a0be3937d618a27.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{15}{17}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{15}{17})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{225}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{17}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{15}{17})}{(\\frac{8}{17})}=\\frac{15}{8}.\n$$" "![](images/ba/bad705982f073623644ed540e26cfad849b2dd078c5c64772a0be3937d618a27.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{15}{17}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{15}{17}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{225}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{17}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{15}{17}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{8}{17}\\right)}=\\frac{15}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511461 :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=-3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)(x+3)}{2k(x+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)\\cancel{(x+3)}}{2k\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{x+11}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(-\\frac{kx}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{k(-3)}{3} \\\\  & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(-3)=k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-3,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-2$ or $k=2$ remove the discontinuity at $x=-3.$" "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=-3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)(x+3)}{2k(x+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)\\cancel{(x+3)}}{2k\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{x+11}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}\\left(-\\frac{kx}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{k(-3)}{3} \\\\  & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(-3)=k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-3,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-2$ or $k=2$ remove the discontinuity at $x=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511492 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{\\theta })}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}+1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\theta }\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}+1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511494 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511532 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511532 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511541 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta })^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta })^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511546 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{\\pi /4}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{\\pi /4}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511574 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=10$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{25}$ | $\\frac{23}{25}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ | $\\frac{2}{25}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=10$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{20}{23}$ | $\\frac{3}{23}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=6] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,6)]-{\\mu }_{Y|6}^{2} \\\\  & =[{5}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+{10}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}]-{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}+50-\\frac{225}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=10$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{25}$ | $\\frac{23}{25}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ | $\\frac{2}{25}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=10$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{20}{23}$ | $\\frac{3}{23}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=6] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,6)]-{\\mu }_{Y|6}^{2} \\\\  & =[{5}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+{10}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}]-{\\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}+50-\\frac{225}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511686 :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/608cde6d839f956b950438c50f54338fb7ec7b816ffc21ac38515dbf79a9f980.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{7}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{7}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{49}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$" "![](images/60/608cde6d839f956b950438c50f54338fb7ec7b816ffc21ac38515dbf79a9f980.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{7}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{49}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511687 :question/worked-solution "![](images/a6/a6a3acf1948838a3d1d8f0effe0e1cdb30282b289f89009cbbcac716b4b73700.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{6}{25}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{38}{25}.$" "![](images/a6/a6a3acf1948838a3d1d8f0effe0e1cdb30282b289f89009cbbcac716b4b73700.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{6}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{38}{25}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511701 :question/worked-solution "![](images/69/69ab1b2e23a2815343db2b914204aa5864e73254dd1aa22d501c08b8ae155714.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{17}{49}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{34}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{64}{49}.$" "![](images/69/69ab1b2e23a2815343db2b914204aa5864e73254dd1aa22d501c08b8ae155714.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{17}{49}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{34}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{64}{49}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511705 :question/worked-solution "![](images/df/df6444faa947e137efc95beeb24c59e8e685830096bf4019fef776abd87c4bff.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{6}{36}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}.$" "![](images/df/df6444faa947e137efc95beeb24c59e8e685830096bf4019fef776abd87c4bff.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{6}{36}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511708 :question/worked-solution "![](images/09/0961629419accd50af36ce88aecafe33ea264d278d18d6f71c4aa4d76c06be97.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{13}{25}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{26}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{24}{25}.$" "![](images/09/0961629419accd50af36ce88aecafe33ea264d278d18d6f71c4aa4d76c06be97.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{13}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{26}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{24}{25}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511918 :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\cos x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos x=\\cos (x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos x=\\cos (x+2\\cdot (-7)\\cdot \\pi )=\\cos (x-14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos x=\\frac{1}{7},$ then $\\cos (x-14\\pi )=\\frac{1}{7}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cos (x-14\\pi )+2 & =2(\\frac{1}{7})+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The period of $y=\\cos x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos x=\\cos (x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos x=\\cos (x+2\\cdot (-7)\\cdot \\pi )=\\cos (x-14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos x=\\frac{1}{7},$ then $\\cos (x-14\\pi )=\\frac{1}{7}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cos (x-14\\pi )+2 & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511924 :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\cos x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos x=\\cos (x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos x=\\cos (x+2\\cdot 5\\cdot \\pi )=\\cos (x+10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos x=\\frac{5}{7},$ then $\\cos (x+10\\pi )=\\frac{5}{7}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\cos (x+10\\pi )+2 & =-4(\\frac{5}{7})+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{7}+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The period of $y=\\cos x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos x=\\cos (x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos x=\\cos (x+2\\cdot 5\\cdot \\pi )=\\cos (x+10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos x=\\frac{5}{7},$ then $\\cos (x+10\\pi )=\\frac{5}{7}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\cos (x+10\\pi )+2 & =-4\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{7}+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511963 :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\cos x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos x=\\cos (x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-8$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos x=\\cos (x+2\\cdot (-8)\\cdot \\pi )=\\cos (x-16\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos x=-\\frac{2}{11},$ then $\\cos (x-16\\pi )=-\\frac{2}{11}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cos (x-16\\pi )-2 & =6(-\\frac{2}{11})-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{11}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{34}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The period of $y=\\cos x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos x=\\cos (x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-8$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos x=\\cos (x+2\\cdot (-8)\\cdot \\pi )=\\cos (x-16\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos x=-\\frac{2}{11},$ then $\\cos (x-16\\pi )=-\\frac{2}{11}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cos (x-16\\pi )-2 & =6\\left(-\\frac{2}{11}\\right)-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{11}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{34}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511973 :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\cos x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos x=\\cos (x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos x=\\cos (x+2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\pi )=\\cos (x+4\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos x=\\frac{2}{11},$ then $\\cos (x+4\\pi )=\\frac{2}{11}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cos (x+4\\pi )+4 & =-5(\\frac{2}{11})+4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{11}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The period of $y=\\cos x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos x=\\cos (x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos x=\\cos (x+2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\pi )=\\cos (x+4\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos x=\\frac{2}{11},$ then $\\cos (x+4\\pi )=\\frac{2}{11}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cos (x+4\\pi )+4 & =-5\\left(\\frac{2}{11}\\right)+4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{11}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186511995 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$” is true. At $x=0$, the function has value $\\cos 0=1$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a maximum value at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$” is false. $\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{2})=0$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a maximum value at $x=\\pi.$” is false. $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$." "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$” is true. At $x=0$, the function has value $\\cos 0=1$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a maximum value at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$” is false. $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=0$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a maximum value at $x=\\pi.$” is false. $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$."] [:db/cas 17592186512001 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$” is true. At $x=0$, the function has value $\\cos 0=1$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a maximum value at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$” is false. $\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{2})=0$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a minimum value at $x=\\pi.$” is true. $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$." "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$” is true. At $x=0$, the function has value $\\cos 0=1$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a maximum value at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$” is false. $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=0$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a minimum value at $x=\\pi.$” is true. $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$."] [:db/cas 17592186512008 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=0.$” is false. At $x=0$, the function has value $\\cos 0=1$.\n\n- Statement “The function is zero at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$” is true. $\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{2})=0$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a minimum value at $x=\\pi.$” is true. $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$." "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=0.$” is false. At $x=0$, the function has value $\\cos 0=1$.\n\n- Statement “The function is zero at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$” is true. $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=0$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a minimum value at $x=\\pi.$” is true. $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$."] [:db/cas 17592186512037 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$” is true. At $x=0$, the function has value $\\cos 0=1$.\n\n- Statement “The function is zero at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$” is true. $\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{2})=0$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a minimum value at $x=\\pi.$” is true. $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$." "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$” is true. At $x=0$, the function has value $\\cos 0=1$.\n\n- Statement “The function is zero at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$” is true. $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=0$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a minimum value at $x=\\pi.$” is true. $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$."] [:db/cas 17592186512050 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$” is true. At $x=0$, the function has value $\\cos 0=1$.\n\n- Statement “The function is zero at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$” is true. $\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{2})=0$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a maximum value at $x=\\pi.$” is false. $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$." "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$” is true. At $x=0$, the function has value $\\cos 0=1$.\n\n- Statement “The function is zero at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$” is true. $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=0$.\n\n- Statement “The function has a maximum value at $x=\\pi.$” is false. $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$."] [:db/cas 17592186512880 :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}({x}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y[\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2}{25}y+\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{25}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{15}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{75}-(\\frac{14}{25})(\\frac{19}{15}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}({x}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y[\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2}{25}y+\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{25}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{15}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{75}-\\left(\\frac{14}{25}\\right)\\left(\\frac{19}{15}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186512888 :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}+x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y[\\frac{32}{5}+2{y}^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2}{5}y+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{40}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}-(\\frac{13}{10})(\\frac{13}{10}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{100}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}+x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y[\\frac{32}{5}+2{y}^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2}{5}y+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{40}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}-\\left(\\frac{13}{10}\\right)\\left(\\frac{13}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{100}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186512917 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=$" "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186512917 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{1/2}}={e}^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{1/2}}={e}^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\right)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186512930 :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{{field-2}}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{{field-2}}\\cdot sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186512930 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n})cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n})sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n} &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${b}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\pi }{15}\\cdot \\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$" "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute 