}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186512941 :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})(\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right)\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186513314 :answer/value "y={(\\frac{39}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{23}{5}" "y={\\left(\\frac{39}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{23}{5}"] [:db/cas 17592186513327 :question/problem "![](images/99/99451d92983062f0741cb5b2f01c5b30f91ddfc7508d5c6c61d31caed77295c4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{39}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." "![](images/99/99451d92983062f0741cb5b2f01c5b30f91ddfc7508d5c6c61d31caed77295c4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{39}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve."] [:db/cas 17592186513327 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{39}{2})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={(\\frac{39}{2})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-\\frac{23}{5}.$ Therefore, $c=-\\frac{23}{5}.$" "The horizontal asymptote of $y={\\left(\\frac{39}{2}\\right)}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={\\left(\\frac{39}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-\\frac{23}{5}.$ Therefore, $c=-\\frac{23}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186513330 :answer/value "y={(\\frac{39}{2})}^{x}-\\frac{18}{5}" "y={\\left(\\frac{39}{2}\\right)}^{x}-\\frac{18}{5}"] [:db/cas 17592186513336 :answer/value "y={(\\frac{39}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{18}{5}" "y={\\left(\\frac{39}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{18}{5}"] [:db/cas 17592186513340 :answer/value "y={(\\frac{39}{2})}^{x}-\\frac{23}{5}" "y={\\left(\\frac{39}{2}\\right)}^{x}-\\frac{23}{5}"] [:db/cas 17592186513344 :answer/value 