003de162949be163c9e95347c8003907896f033a75dd759755eca1.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{x}-\\frac{9}{4},$ we take the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{x}$ and shift it $\\frac{9}{4}$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,- \\frac{5}{4}),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=- \\frac{9}{4}.$\n\n![](images/11/1137ee2e0adbdc21f9fbb1a08a77c8977a2f8e85e561cb4f267ef1654eda4c8e.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186513488 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}-3?$" "Which of the following is the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}-3?$"] [:db/cas 17592186513488 :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/d6/d648afa4906f2e2dc6661e3020e83eca28724dee7b46ddc61c36373584e8cb95.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}-3,$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}$ and shift it $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/57/57ad3064c37dad412554f92b4ec781c746df68bab3e02a584b123c20b71fade2.png)" "First, we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/d6/d648afa4906f2e2dc6661e3020e83eca28724dee7b46ddc61c36373584e8cb95.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}-3,$ we take the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}$ and shift it $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/57/57ad3064c37dad412554f92b4ec781c746df68bab3e02a584b123c20b71fade2.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186513582 :answer/value "{log}_{1/16}⁡(\\frac{1}{16})=0" "{log}_{1/16}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)=0"] [:db/cas 17592186513717 :answer/value "{log}_{16}⁡(\\frac{1}{16})=1" "{log}_{16}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)=1"] [:db/cas 17592186513745 :answer/value "{log}_{16}⁡(\\frac{1}{16})=0" "{log}_{16}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)=0"] [:db/cas 17592186514008 :answer/value "{log}_{1/16}⁡(\\frac{1}{16})=1" "{log}_{1/16}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)=1"] [:db/cas 17592186514476 :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{16})}^{1}=\\frac{1}{16}$ can be expressed as" "${\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{1}{16}$ can be expressed as"] [:db/cas 17592186514476 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${(\\frac{1}{16})}^{1}=\\frac{1}{16}$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=\\frac{1}{16},b=\\frac{1}{16}$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${(\\frac{1}{16})}^{1}=\\frac{1}{16}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{1/16}⁡(\\frac{1}{16})=1.$\n\nNote that ${log}_{n}⁡n=1$ for any $n>0,n\\ne 1.$" "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{1}{16}$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=\\frac{1}{16},b=\\frac{1}{16}$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{1}{16}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{1/16}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)=1.$\n\nNote that ${log}_{n}⁡n=1$ for any $n>0,n\\ne 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186513676 :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{27}{125})=\\frac{5}{3}" "{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{27}{125}\\right)=\\frac{5}{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186513747 :answer/value "{log}_{3/5}⁡(\\frac{27}{125})=-3" "{log}_{3/5}⁡\\left(\\frac{27}{125}\\right)=-3"] [:db/cas 17592186514092 :answer/value "{log}_{5/3}⁡(\\frac{27}{125})=-3" "{log}_{5/3}⁡\\left(\\frac{27}{125}\\right)=-3"] [:db/cas 17592186514382 :question/problem "${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{-3}=\\frac{27}{125}$ can be expressed as" "${\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{-3}=\\frac{27}{125}$ can be expressed as"] [:db/cas 17592186514382 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{-3}=\\frac{27}{125}$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=\\frac{27}{125},b=\\frac{5}{3}$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as ${log}_{5/3}⁡(\\frac{27}{125})=-3.$" "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{-3}=\\frac{27}{125}$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=\\frac{27}{125},b=\\frac{5}{3}$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as ${log}_{5/3}⁡\\left(\\frac{27}{125}\\right)=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186513779 :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{27})=-3" "{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)=-3"] [:db/cas 17592186513991 :answer/value "{log}_{1/3}⁡(\\frac{1}{27})=-3" "{log}_{1/3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)=-3"] [:db/cas 17592186514456 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${3}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{27}$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=\\frac{1}{27},b=3$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as ${log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{27})=-3.$" "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${3}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{27}$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=\\frac{1}{27},b=3$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as ${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186513868 :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})≈1.587" "{log}_{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈1.587"] [:db/cas 17592186514116 :answer/value "{log}_{1.587}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})≈4" "{log}_{1.587}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈4"] [:db/cas 17592186513880 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})≈1.414" "{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈1.414"] [:db/cas 17592186514383 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514388 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514390 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{(\\frac{5}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514391 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514399 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=-1.$\n\nApplying the root rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to -2}(\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)^{3}-3(-2)^{2}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{-4-12}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=-1.$\n\nApplying the root rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\left(\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)^{3}-3(-2)^{2}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{-4-12}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514422 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=${{field-1}}" "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right)\\,=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186514422 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514428 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=13$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{13+1}}{13+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{14}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{14})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{14}\\cdot 14\\,{x}^{13} \\\\ &= {x}^{13}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=13$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{13+1}}{13+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{14}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{14})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{14}\\cdot 14\\,{x}^{13} \\\\ &= {x}^{13}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514440 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(-\\frac{3}{2})$ is $0.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $2.$" "As $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ is $0.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186514446 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=4$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4+1}}{4+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\ &= {x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=4$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4+1}}{4+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\ &= {x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514703 :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $8x+6y=-19,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+6y & =-19 \\\\ 6y & =-8x-19 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{8}{6}x-\\frac{19}{6} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{19}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1$ has a slope of $\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1$ is perpendicular to the given line." "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $8x+6y=-19,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+6y & =-19 \\\\ 6y & =-8x-19 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{8}{6}x-\\frac{19}{6} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{19}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1$ has a slope of $\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1$ is perpendicular to the given line."] [:db/cas 17592186514709 :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $-5x+2y=4,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+2y & =4 \\\\ 2y & =5x+4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-\\frac{2}{5}x-7$ has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5})=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-\\frac{2}{5}x-7$ is perpendicular to the given line." "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $-5x+2y=4,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+2y & =4 \\\\ 2y & =5x+4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-\\frac{2}{5}x-7$ has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right)=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-\\frac{2}{5}x-7$ is perpendicular to the given line."] [:db/cas 17592186514712 :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $-6x+9y=-13,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x+9y & =-13 \\\\ 9y & =6x-13 \\\\ y & =\\frac{6}{9}x-\\frac{13}{9} \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{13}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-co