ince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{6}{5}x+3$ is perpendicular to the given line."] [:db/cas 17592186514739 :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $-18x-16y=18,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-18x-16y & =18 \\\\ -16y & =18x+18 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{18}{16}x-\\frac{18}{16} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{9}{8}x-\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{9}{8}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{8}{9}x-7$ has a slope of $\\frac{8}{9}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{9}{8})\\cdot \\frac{8}{9}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{8}{9}x-7$ is perpendicular to the given line." "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $-18x-16y=18,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-18x-16y & =18 \\\\ -16y & =18x+18 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{18}{16}x-\\frac{18}{16} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{9}{8}x-\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{9}{8}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{8}{9}x-7$ has a slope of $\\frac{8}{9}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{9}{8}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{8}{9}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{8}{9}x-7$ is perpendicular to the given line."] [:db/cas 17592186514741 :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $-10x+16y=-4,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10x+16y & =-4 \\\\ 16y & =10x-4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{10}{16}x-\\frac{4}{16} \\\\ y & =\\frac