{\\int }_{5}^{49}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{49}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{u}}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\right]_{5}^{49} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{2}\\right]_{5}^{49} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(49\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(5\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{343}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{343}{2} - \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186519969 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2 t + 2,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6 t \\\\  & =6 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6 y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\left(6 t\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6 \\sqrt{5} t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\left[3 \