scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{3} - x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,(- x^{3} - x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530714 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- x - 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x - 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{3} - x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,(- x^{3} - x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- x - 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x - 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{3} - x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,(- x^{3} - x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530725 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2})=4 x + y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2})=x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(4 x + y)^2+(x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 2 x (2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2}) - y (2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 4 x^{4} y - 4 x^{3} y^{2} - 4 x^{3} - 9 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 4 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1} (- 4 x^{4} y - 4 x^{3} y^{2} - 4 x^{3} - 9 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 4 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2})=4 x + y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2})=x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(4 x + y)^2+(x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 2 x (2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2}) - y (2 x^{2} + x y + 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 4 x^{4} y - 4 x^{3} y^{2} - 4 x^{3} - 9 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 4 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{(x + 4 y)^{2} + (4 x + y)^{2} + 1} (- 4 x^{4} y - 4 x^{3} y^{2} - 4 x^{3} - 9 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 4 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530730 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- x - 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x - 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2}) - y (- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} - 5 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1} (- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} - 5 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})=- x - 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x - 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2}) - y (- x^{2} - x y - 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} - 5 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x - 4 y)^{2} + 1} (- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} - 5 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530731 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2}) - y (- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} + 3 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y + 4 x y^{4} + 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1} (- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} + 3 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y + 4 x y^{4} + 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2}) - y (- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} + 3 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y + 4 x y^{4} + 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1} (- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} + 3 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y + 4 x y^{4} + 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530732 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=- 4 x + 2 y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=2 x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 4 x + 2 y)^2+(2 x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x (- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=- 4 x + 2 y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=2 x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 4 x + 2 y)^2+(2 x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x (- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531565 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={3}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{({3}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{3}^{2\\theta }+{3}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡3){3}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{3}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {3}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({3}^{\\pi }-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={3}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{({3}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{3}^{2\\theta }+{3}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡3){3}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{3}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {3}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({3}^{\\pi }-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531574 :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-5{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}y({y}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}({y}^{3}-5{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-5{\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}-\\frac{5}{6}{y}^{6}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{25}-0)-\\frac{5}{6}(\\frac{1}{125}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{100}-\\frac{1}{150}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{300}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{150}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-5{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}y({y}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}({y}^{3}-5{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-5{\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}-\\frac{5}{6}{y}^{6}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{5}}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{25}-0)-\\frac{5}{6}(\\frac{1}{125}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{100}-\\frac{1}{150}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{300}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{150}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531575 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-3{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -3[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2b \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2b+1 \\\\ 2a+5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2b+1=-3a \\\\ 2a+5=-3b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n-2b+1=-3a\\,⇒\\,a=\\frac{2b-1}{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n2(\\frac{2b-1}{3})+5=-3b\\,⇒\\,4b-2+15=-9b\\,⇒\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na=\\frac{2(-1)-1}{3}=\\frac{-2-1}{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-3{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -3[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2b \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2b+1 \\\\ 2a+5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2b+1=-3a \\\\ 2a+5=-3b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n-2b+1=-3a\\,⇒\\,a=\\frac{2b-1}{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\frac{2b-1}{3}\\right)+5=-3b\\,⇒\\,4b-2+15=-9b\\,⇒\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na=\\frac{2(-1)-1}{3}=\\frac{-2-1}{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531587 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t+9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot |3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t+9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot |3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531606 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6{e}^{3\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=18{e}^{3\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(6{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}+{(18{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{36{e}^{6\\theta }+324{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{360{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{e}^{3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{10}{[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}{[{e}^{3\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}({e}^{\\pi }-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6{e}^{3\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=18{e}^{3\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(6{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}+{(18{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{36{e}^{6\\theta }+324{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{360{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{e}^{3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{10}{[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}{[{e}^{3\\theta }]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}({e}^{\\pi }-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531612 :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-2y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,1-2y=0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}y({y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}({y}^{3}-2{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{16}-0)-\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{32}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{80}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{320}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{160}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-2y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,1-2y=0 \\\\ y & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}y({y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}({y}^{3}-2{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}\\,) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{16}-0)-\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{32}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{80}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{320}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{160}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531626 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531628 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}3\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}3\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531644 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={2}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({2}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{2}^{2\\theta }+{2}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2){2}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{2}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {2}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({2}^{\\pi /2}-{2}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={2}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({2}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{2}^{2\\theta }+{2}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2){2}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{2}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {2}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({2}^{\\pi /2}-{2}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531731 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(3,-4,6)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,-4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,-4) & =\\frac{5(3)}{(-4)}-5(3)^2(-4)+2\\cos(\\pi (-4)) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{15}{4})+(180)+(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{713}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(3,-4,6)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,-4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,-4) & =\\frac{5(3)}{(-4)}-5(3)^2(-4)+2\\cos(\\pi (-4)) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{15}{4}\\right)+(180)+(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{713}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531741 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,-3,-8)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluat